== S1
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Functions of a complex variable}

=== 1
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Laurent series> is
$$
e^{-1/z}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{n!}z^{-n}=1-\frac1z+\frac1{2z^2}-\frac1{6z^3}+\cdots,
\qquad z\ne0.
$$
Its <principal part> contains infinitely many nonzero negative powers, so **zero is an essential singularity**. The first three displayed terms are not an asymptotic truncation as $z\to0$; they are the beginning of the convergent <Laurent series> in powers of $1/z$.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

The coefficient of $z^{-1}$ in the <Laurent series> is $-1$. The <residue theorem> therefore gives, for the standard positive orientation,
$$
\boxed{\oint_Ce^{-1/z}\,dz=2\pi i\operatorname{Res}_{z=0}e^{-1/z}=-2\pi i.}
$$
The <residue theorem> applies to an isolated <essential singularity> as well as to a <pole>.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

Parametrize the positively oriented unit <circle> by $z=e^{i\theta}$. Then $e^{-1/z}=e^{-\cos\theta+i\sin\theta}$ and $dz=ie^{i\theta}d\theta$, so
$$
\oint_Ce^{-1/z}dz
=i\int_0^{2\pi}e^{-\cos\theta}\cos(\theta+\sin\theta)d\theta
-\int_0^{2\pi}e^{-\cos\theta}\sin(\theta+\sin\theta)d\theta.
$$
Equating the <real part> and <imaginary part> with $-2\pi i$ gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{2\pi}e^{-\cos\theta}\cos(\theta+\sin\theta)d\theta=-2\pi,\qquad
\int_0^{2\pi}e^{-\cos\theta}\sin(\theta+\sin\theta)d\theta=0.}
$$

=== 2
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Joukowski map> $f(z)=z+z^{-1}$ is <holomorphic> on $\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$. A <holomorphic function> is locally <conformal> where its <derivative> is nonzero. Here $f'(z)=1-z^{-2}$ vanishes at $z=\pm1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{holomorphic on }\mathbb C\setminus\{0\};\qquad
\text{conformal at every point except }0,1,-1,}
$$
where the exclusion of zero is because the function is undefined there. Holomorphy alone does not supply conformality at the two <critical points>.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Put $z=1+\zeta$. Expanding the <holomorphic function> near its <critical point> gives
$$
f(1+\zeta)=2+\zeta^2-\zeta^3+O(\zeta^4).
$$
For a small ray $\zeta=re^{i\theta}$, the leading image displacement is $r^2e^{2i\theta}$. Thus **oriented ray angles are doubled modulo $2\pi$** at $z=1$. This is <angle multiplication at a holomorphic critical point> of multiplicity two; an unoriented angle between full tangent lines must also be interpreted modulo $\pi$. The <map> is not locally one-to-one there.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

For $z=e^{i\theta}$ the image is $w=2\cos\theta$, a real number. Therefore **each semicircle maps onto the real interval $[-2,2]$**, with endpoints included for closed semicircles. Under positive traversal of the full unit <circle>, the upper half runs from $2$ to $-2$ and the lower half returns from $-2$ to $2$. Every interior point has two unit-circle preimages. The <Joukowski circle-to-ellipse mapping> degenerates to a doubled line segment at this radius.

==== d
{scope}

===== Solution

For a radius $\rho>0$, write $z=\rho e^{i\theta}$. Then
$$
w=(\rho+\rho^{-1})\cos\theta+i(\rho-\rho^{-1})\sin\theta.
$$
For $\rho\ne1$ its image is the <ellipse>
$$
\boxed{\frac{(\operatorname{Re}w)^2}{(\rho+\rho^{-1})^2}
+\frac{(\operatorname{Im}w)^2}{(\rho-\rho^{-1})^2}=1.}
$$
Its horizontal semiaxis is $\rho+\rho^{-1}$, its vertical semiaxis is $|\rho-\rho^{-1}|$, and its <foci> are $\pm2$ because the squared semiaxes differ by $4$. Reciprocal radii give the same <ellipse>, traversed in opposite directions as $\theta$ increases.

\Image[2012-s1-joukowski-circles.png]
{title=The Joukowski map sends reciprocal-radius circles to one ellipse and the unit circle to a doubled interval}

=== 3
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

The <square-root branch points> are **zero and infinity**. At zero the local behavior is $z^{-1/2}$; in the coordinate $u=1/z$ near infinity it is $u^{1/2}$. In either case one circuit reverses the sign and two restore it. Thus the <ramification index> is $2$: these are two-sheeted algebraic <branch points>, sometimes described as branch order one when order means ramification index minus one. The decay to zero at infinity does not remove the branching.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

TODO: the original closed-contour drawing is absent from the source. The following independent <Bromwich contour> deformation establishes the required discontinuity relation without asserting the missing drawing's exact geometry. Orient the vertical line upward, as required by the positive imaginary answer in the next part, and use the <principal square root>, $-\pi<\arg z<\pi$.

Close a truncated vertical line to the left with a rectangle, slit it along the negative real axis, and exclude a small circle about zero. The integrand is <holomorphic> inside that slit domain, so the boundary <contour integral> is zero. Horizontal boundary integrals are $O(R^{-1/2})$: integrating $e^x$ along $-R\le x\le1$ gives a bounded factor while $|z|^{-1/2}\le R^{-1/2}$. The left boundary contributes $O(e^{-R}R^{1/2})$, and the small circle contributes $O(\sqrt\varepsilon)$. All vanish in the indicated limits.

At $z=-x$ with $x>0$, the upper and lower bank values are respectively $-ie^{-x}/\sqrt x$ and $+ie^{-x}/\sqrt x$. The boundary follows the upper bank toward zero and the lower bank away from zero. Their combined contribution is $-2i\int_0^\infty e^{-x}x^{-1/2}dx$. Hence
$$
\boxed{I=2i\int_0^\infty e^{-x}x^{-1/2}dx.}
$$
The vertically improper integral converges conditionally; these estimates also justify the contour limit. Reversing the vertical orientation reverses its sign.

\Image[2012-s1-independent-bromwich-contour.png]
{title=An independently chosen slit-rectangle contour relates the upward Bromwich integral to the square-root branch jump}

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Gamma function> identity $\Gamma(1/2)=\sqrt\pi$ gives the <Bromwich integral of an inverse square root> directly:
$$
\boxed{I=2i\Gamma(1/2)=2i\sqrt\pi.}
$$
Equivalently, put $x=u^2$ in the real <integral>. It becomes $2\int_0^\infty e^{-u^2}du=\sqrt\pi$ by the <Gaussian integral>.

== S2
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Astrophysics: from planets to the cosmos}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

For two bodies of masses $M_1,M_2$, the relative <Kepler orbit> has semimajor axis $a$ and orbital <period> $P$ satisfying <Kepler's third law>
$$
\boxed{P^2=\frac{4\pi^2a^3}{G(M_1+M_2)}.}
$$
Here $a$ is the semimajor axis of the separation vector, not either star's individual barycentric orbit. In conventional stellar units the same relation is approximately $(P/\mathrm{yr})^2=(a/\mathrm{AU})^3/[(M_1+M_2)/M_\odot]$, with year, <astronomical unit> and <solar mass> as the units.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Equal <radial-velocity> amplitudes imply equal stellar masses. Under the circular, edge-on <eclipsing binary> interpretation, each star moves at $K=100\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$, and the relative speed is $2K$. Thus
$$
a=\frac{(K_1+K_2)P}{2\pi}=1.499\times10^{10}\,\mathrm m,
\qquad
M_1+M_2=\frac{P(K_1+K_2)^3}{2\pi G}.
$$
Using $P=5.45$ days gives
$$
\boxed{a\simeq0.100\,\mathrm{AU},\qquad M_1=M_2\simeq2.26M_\odot.}
$$
More generally, <spectroscopic binary> amplitudes determine $a\sin i=P(K_1+K_2)\sqrt{1-e^2}/(2\pi)$ and $(M_1+M_2)\sin^3i=P(K_1+K_2)^3(1-e^2)^{3/2}/(2\pi G)$. Thus these numerical masses and separation use $e=0$, $\sin i=1$; eclipses alone imply high inclination rather than its exact value.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

TODO: the source light curve, its numerical contact phases and the meanings of the four plotted time labels are absent, so unique numerical stellar radii cannot be recovered. The <central eclipse contact-radius relations> explain what the missing measurements would determine.

If $t_1,\ldots,t_4$ denote the usual first through fourth contacts of a central eclipse, define $T_{14}=t_4-t_1$ and $T_{23}=t_3-t_2$, and let $R_L\ge R_S$ be the two radii. For a circular edge-on orbit,
$$
R_L+R_S=a\sin\frac{\pi T_{14}}P,\qquad
R_L-R_S=a\sin\frac{\pi T_{23}}P.
$$
Taking half their sum and difference gives the radii. For short eclipses the familiar approximation is $R_L\simeq v_{\rm rel}(T_{14}+T_{23})/4$ and $R_S\simeq v_{\rm rel}(T_{14}-T_{23})/4$, with $v_{\rm rel}=2K$. At first and fourth contact the discs touch externally; at second and third the smaller disc is just internally tangent to the larger. Noncentral eclipses require the projected impact parameter as well.

\Image[2012-s2-central-eclipse-contacts.png]
{title=Conditional central-eclipse contact geometry; this schematic supplies no readings from the missing source light curve}

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Build the star by adding spherical shells. With $m(r)$ the enclosed <mass>, its <gravitational binding energy> is
$$
\Omega=-\int_0^{M_\odot}\frac{Gm}{r(m)}\,dm.
$$
For a nondegenerate extended star, $r(m)<R_\odot$ on the interior, so
$$
\boxed{|\Omega|>\frac{G}{R_\odot}\int_0^{M_\odot}m\,dm
=\frac{GM_\odot^2}{2R_\odot}.}
$$
This is a <gravitational binding-energy lower bound for a spherical star>. The <Kelvin-Helmholtz cooling time> is of order
$$
\boxed{\tau_{\rm KH}\sim\frac{GM_\odot^2}{R_\odot L_\odot}\simeq3.1\times10^7\,\mathrm{yr}.}
$$
The displayed energy bound gives $|\Omega|/L_\odot>1.6\times10^7$ years; it supplies a lower bound on that reservoir-to-luminosity ratio. A stellar structure model sets its coefficient. For an ideal monatomic, gas-supported star the <virial theorem> puts about half the released gravitational energy into heat, leaving half available for radiation; a uniform-density model would give about $9\times10^6$ years. These contraction estimates are all of order $10^7$ years, much shorter than the nuclear lifetime.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the supplied atomic masses consistently. Their <mass defect> per four hydrogen atoms is $4(1.0078)-4.0026=0.0286$ atomic mass units. The conversion efficiency is
$$
\boxed{\epsilon=\frac{4m_H-m_{\rm He}}{4m_H}=0.007095\simeq0.71\%.}
$$
The atomic-mass bookkeeping includes the associated positron-annihilation energy; an exact photon-heating budget also subtracts energy carried away by <neutrinos>. With the stated ten-percent fuel fraction and constant present <solar luminosity>, the <main-sequence> lifetime estimate is
$$
\boxed{\tau_{\rm MS}\simeq\frac{0.1\epsilon M_\odot c^2}{L_\odot}\simeq1.05\times10^{10}\,\mathrm{yr}.}
$$
The much longer timescale explains why <stellar nuclear fusion> can power the Sun throughout its long main-sequence history.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

A <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram> plots <stellar luminosity> upward and <effective temperature> increasing toward the left. The <main sequence> runs from hot luminous stars at upper left to cool faint stars at lower right, with the Sun near $T_{\rm eff}\simeq5800\,\mathrm K$, $L=L_\odot$.

In the <evolution of a solar-mass star>, core hydrogen fusion initially supplies the luminosity. As core hydrogen is exhausted, hydrogen-shell fusion around a contracting helium core carries the star through the <subgiant> and <red giant> phases toward higher luminosity and lower surface temperature. Helium ignition is followed by core helium fusion with a hydrogen-burning shell, placing a solar-mass star in the <red clump>. The later <asymptotic giant branch> has hydrogen- and helium-burning shells around a carbon-oxygen core. Envelope ejection exposes a hot central star of a <planetary nebula>; residual shell burning ends and the resulting <white dwarf> radiates stored thermal energy as it cools.

\Image[2012-s2-solar-evolution-hr.png]
{title=Schematic solar-mass evolution on an H-R diagram, with the dominant energy sources labelled}

=== 3
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

Thermal <interstellar dust> produces a bright <galactic foreground> concentrated toward the <Milky Way> plane, with additional cloud structure. Its emission spectrum differs from the approximately $2.73\,\mathrm K$ <blackbody> spectrum of the <cosmic microwave background>, so comparisons across <frequencies> help separate it. In the sketch below this is the bright equatorial band.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Observer motion produces the <cosmic microwave background dipole>: to first order in $\beta=v/c$,
$$
\frac{\Delta T(\theta)}{T_0}=\beta\cos\theta.
$$
The direction of motion is hotter and the opposite direction cooler. Its full-sky pattern has one broad positive hemisphere and one broad negative hemisphere; it is not concentrated in the <galactic plane>.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

After subtracting the mean, the motion-induced dipole and <galactic foregrounds>, intrinsic <cosmic microwave background anisotropy> appears as angular hot and cold patches, typically at the $10^{-5}$ fractional scale. These spatial <temperature fluctuations> encode early density and velocity structure. The displayed patch pattern is illustrative, with no claim to reproduce an observed sky map.

\Image[2012-s2-microwave-sky-components.png]
{title=Schematic microwave components: Galactic dust, a kinematic dipole and intrinsic anisotropy, using separate colour scales}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Measure the absolute <spectral radiance> over a range of <frequencies> and fit the <Planck spectrum>
$$
B_\nu(T)=\frac{2h\nu^3}{c^2}\frac1{e^{h\nu/(k_BT)}-1}.
$$
For example, the <COBE FIRAS blackbody spectrum experiment> used a differential <Michelson interferometer> to compare the sky with an internal <blackbody>; an external calibrated <blackbody> could cover the sky entrance. Cold <bolometers> measured the difference spectrum, and foreground separation distinguished <Milky Way> emission from the nearly isotropic background. The fitted background temperature is approximately $\boxed{T_0=2.73\,\mathrm K}$.<The COBE Far Infrared Absolute Spectrophotometer by NASA LAMBDA>[Instrument overview: differential internal reference, external calibrator and absolute CMB temperature]{ref} The result relies on an absolute spectral comparison, rather than merely measuring a map's hot-to-cold contrast.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The stated one-sided temperature contrast is $\beta=0.00105$, so the <Doppler dipole> gives
$$
\boxed{v=0.00105c\simeq3.15\times10^5\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}=315\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
The motion is **toward the hotter region**. The contrast already gives one side relative to the mean; the hot-to-cold difference would be twice as large.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Adiabatic expansion preserves the <blackbody> form while each photon's <energy> redshifts as $a^{-1}$. Thus the <cosmic microwave background temperature> obeys $T(z)=T_0(1+z)$, giving
$$
\boxed{T(z=3)=4T_0\simeq10.9\,\mathrm K.}
$$

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Convert both inputs to <energy density>. The matter contribution, including rest energy, is $u_{M,0}=\rho_{M,0}c^2=2.25\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}$. The given photon value is $u_{\gamma,0}=5\times10^5(1.602\times10^{-19})=8.01\times10^{-14}\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{u_{M,0}}{u_{\gamma,0}}\simeq2.80\times10^3.}
$$
Matter <energy density> scales as $(1+z)^3$, whereas the <cosmic microwave background> scales as $(1+z)^4$. The <photon-only matter-radiation equality> condition is therefore
$$
1+z_{\rm eq}=\frac{u_{M,0}}{u_{\gamma,0}},\qquad
\boxed{z_{\rm eq}\simeq2.8\times10^3.}
$$
This uses the supplied CMB photon density. Additional relativistic species change equality for total radiation.

== S3
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum ideas}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Black-body radiation> and the <photoelectric effect> exposed failures of classical continuous-wave energy exchange. Classical equipartition predicted an unbounded short-wavelength thermal spectrum, the ultraviolet catastrophe. Quantized oscillator energies $E_n=nh\nu$ give the mean excitation energy $h\nu/(e^{h\nu/(k_BT)}-1)$ and suppress high-frequency modes, producing <Planck's law>. In the modern description, thermal radiation consists of <photons> with energy $h\nu$ and Bose occupation numbers.

For the <photoelectric effect>, increasing intensity below a threshold frequency does not eject electrons, whereas the maximum electron <kinetic energy> grows with frequency above threshold. Absorption of one <photon> transfers $h\nu$ to one electron, giving $\boxed{K_{\max}=h\nu-W}$ and threshold $\nu_0=W/h$, where $W$ is the work function. Intensity primarily changes the photon arrival rate and hence the photocurrent in the ordinary single-photon regime. Thus changing energy per quantum and changing the number of quanta have distinct consequences. Historically the thermal derivation first quantized matter's energy exchange; it should not be described as an already complete derivation of modern photon theory.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For a <photon>, $p_\gamma=E/c$ and $\lambda_\gamma=hc/E$. The <neutron> is nonrelativistic here because $18.8\,\mathrm{keV}\ll m_nc^2$, so $p_n=\sqrt{2m_nE}$ and $\lambda_n=h/p_n$. Using the supplied common energy gives
$$
\boxed{p_\gamma=1.005\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}},\quad
\lambda_\gamma=6.595\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm m,}
$$
$$
\boxed{p_n=3.176\times10^{-21}\,\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}},\quad
\lambda_n=2.086\times10^{-13}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
Equal <kinetic energies> do not imply equal momenta: the massive particle obeys $E=p^2/(2m_n)$, while the massless <photon> obeys $E=pc$. The <neutron> momentum is about $316$ times larger, so its <de Broglie wavelength> is about $316$ times smaller.

To give the <neutron> the photon wavelength, set its momentum to $E/c$, not its <kinetic energy> to $E$ again. Then
$$
\boxed{v_n\simeq\frac{E}{m_nc}=5.999\times10^3\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
Its <kinetic energy> would then be about $0.188\,\mathrm{eV}$, connecting this comparison with the next calculation.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

For a transverse periodic row of scatterers of spacing $d$, normal incidence makes their incident phases equal. Scattering at angle $\theta$ from that normal creates an outgoing path difference $d\sin\theta$ between neighbours. Constructive interference requires a phase difference $2\pi n$, giving
$$
\boxed{d\sin\theta=n\lambda.}
$$
Using this printed relation as the intended transverse <diffraction grating> model, the nonrelativistic <neutron> wavelength at $0.188\,\mathrm{eV}$ is $\lambda=6.5964\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm m$. Hence the first-order data give
$$
\boxed{d=\frac{\lambda}{\sin(0.117)}=5.651\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
**Geometry qualification:** this is not a derivation from reflection at crystal planes parallel to the surface, as the source prose says. The ordinary <Bragg's law> for planes of normal separation $d$ is $2d\sin\theta_B=n\lambda$, where $\theta_B$ is the incident glancing angle to those planes. At normal incidence on parallel planes, $\theta_B=\pi/2$ and specular reflected rays leave along the normal, so their angle from the normal is zero; they cannot obey the printed positive-order relation $d\sin0=n\lambda$ for $\lambda>0$. This is a concrete counterexample to identifying the two geometries. The numerical spacing above is therefore conditional on the printed grating-like relation, rather than a forced assertion that the stated Bragg geometry proves it.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

For nonrelativistic <neutrons>, $\lambda\propto E_K^{-1/2}$, so first-order <uncertainty propagation> gives $|\Delta\lambda|/\lambda=\tfrac12|\Delta E_K|/E_K=0.05$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\Delta\lambda_n\simeq3.30\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
At fixed $d$, differentiating the stipulated first-order relation $d\sin\theta=\lambda$ gives $\Delta\theta=\tan\theta\,\Delta\lambda/\lambda$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\Delta\theta_n\simeq0.00588\,\mathrm{rad}.}
$$
For <photons>, $\lambda\propto E^{-1}$, so the same fractional energy uncertainty gives $\Delta\lambda_\gamma/\lambda_\gamma=0.10$. Their $18.8\,\mathrm{keV}$ wavelength almost equals the low-energy neutron wavelength, so the conditional central angle is $0.116973\,\mathrm{rad}$ and
$$
\boxed{\Delta\theta_\gamma\simeq0.01175\,\mathrm{rad},}
$$
about twice the neutron angular spread. This difference comes from the different energy-momentum relations, not from substantially different central wavelengths. The small-angle approximation is adequate at $0.117\,\mathrm{rad}$: replacing $\sin\theta$ by $\theta$ changes it by about $\theta^2/6=0.23\%$, and replacing $\tan\theta$ by $\theta$ changes the sensitivity by about $0.46\%$. The numerical angular results retain the preceding geometry qualification; a different physical scattering geometry would require its own angle convention.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

In the <Franck-Hertz experiment>, electrons emitted from a heated cathode are accelerated through a dilute atomic vapour toward a collector. A small retarding potential before the collector rejects electrons that have lost too much <kinetic energy>. Below the first excitation threshold, mainly elastic collisions occur and the collector current rises as the accelerating voltage increases. At threshold an inelastic collision can transfer a definite excitation energy to an atom, causing a current drop because the slowed electron cannot overcome the retarding potential.

Raising the voltage further permits acceleration again, and repeated losses produce successive current minima. For mercury their spacing is about $4.9\,\mathrm V$, corresponding to an atomic excitation of about $4.9\,\mathrm{eV}$ and an associated ultraviolet photon near $254\,\mathrm{nm}$. Contact potentials shift the absolute voltages, so the spacing is more informative than the first minimum's position alone. Discrete energy losses support discrete atomic energy levels; a continuously variable excitation energy would not produce the same recurring fixed loss scale. The experiment establishes quantized excitations without requiring the classical electron orbit of the <Bohr model> to be literally correct.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

A classical orbiting electron accelerates and therefore radiates according to the <Larmor formula>. Its mechanical energy becomes more negative, its radius decreases, and the orbit spirals inward rather than remaining a stable atom. The <Bohr model> replaces this prediction with postulated nonradiating stationary states and quantized orbital <angular momentum>, together with photon emission or absorption only between states, $h\nu=|E_n-E_m|$.

For a heavy nucleus, $m_ev r=n\hbar$. Since the <de Broglie wavelength> is $h/(m_ev)$, this implies
$$
\boxed{2\pi r=n\lambda,\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.}
$$
The electronic wave fits an integer number of wavelengths around the orbit. This is a standing-wave consistency interpretation of the quantization rule, not a classical mechanism that prevents an accelerating charge from radiating. For finite nuclear mass, the two-body relative coordinate uses <reduced mass> $\mu=m_eM/(m_e+M)$; the next derivation retains that correction. In the limit $M\gg m_e$, it reduces to the fixed-nucleus electron description above.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $r$ be the electron-nucleus separation and use the <reduced mass> $\mu=m_eM/(m_e+M)$. Coulomb attraction supplies the relative centripetal force, and Bohr quantization fixes the relative <angular momentum>:
$$
\frac{\mu v^2}{r}=\frac{Ze^2}{4\pi\epsilon_0r^2},\qquad \mu vr=n\hbar.
$$
Eliminating $v$ gives
$$
\boxed{r_n=\frac{4\pi\epsilon_0\hbar^2}{\mu Ze^2}n^2
=a_0\frac{m_e}{\mu}\frac{n^2}{Z}.}
$$
The total <energy> is $E=\mu v^2/2-Ze^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)=-Ze^2/(8\pi\epsilon_0r)$, hence
$$
\boxed{E_n=-\frac{\mu Z^2e^4}{2(4\pi\epsilon_0)^2\hbar^2n^2}
=-hcR_M\frac{Z^2}{n^2},\quad R_M=R_\infty\frac\mu{m_e}.}
$$
Here $a_0$ is the ordinary <Bohr radius> and $R_\infty$ the infinite-nuclear-mass <Rydberg constant>. The electron's radius about the centre of mass is $Mr_n/(M+m_e)$; for a heavy nucleus this distinction and the reduced-mass correction are small, but the supplied nuclear mass permits them to be stated precisely.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

The emitted photon carries the decrease in atomic <energy>, so $hc/\lambda=E_n-E_m$ for $n>m$. Using the preceding energy levels gives
$$
\boxed{\frac1\lambda=R_MZ^2\left(\frac1{m^2}-\frac1{n^2}\right).}
$$
The lower state's energy is more negative, making the right-hand side positive. This is the hydrogenic <Rydberg formula>, with the finite-nuclear-mass factor included in $R_M$.

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

At room temperature, $k_BT$ is of order $0.026\,\mathrm{eV}$, far smaller than the electronic excitation gaps of the inferred hydrogenic ion. Ground-state absorption is therefore the natural interpretation. Assign the three lines to $1\to2$, $1\to3$, and $1\to4$. Dividing each reciprocal wavelength by its corresponding factor $1-1/n^2$ gives
$$
R_{\rm eff}=R_MZ^2\simeq(9.8765,9.8684,9.8765)\times10^7\,\mathrm{m^{-1}}.
$$
The small scatter is consistent with the rounded wavelength data. Since $R_\infty\simeq1.0974\times10^7\,\mathrm{m^{-1}}$ and $\mu\simeq m_e$,
$$
\boxed{R_{\rm eff}\simeq9.87\times10^7\,\mathrm{m^{-1}},\qquad Z\simeq\sqrt{R_{\rm eff}/R_\infty}=3.}
$$
The ion is thus hydrogen-like lithium, $\mathrm{Li}^{2+}$. If “effective <Rydberg constant>” denotes only the reduced-mass constant rather than the charge-inclusive spectral coefficient, its value is $R_M\simeq R_{\rm eff}/9\simeq1.097\times10^7\,\mathrm{m^{-1}}$. The two conventions are explicitly distinguished; the rounded lines do not determine a precise isotope mass.

==== 6
{scope}

===== Solution

For Coulomb binding, the <virial theorem> gives a typical <kinetic energy> of the order of the binding energy. Taking $p_{\rm typ}\simeq\sqrt{2m_e|E_1|}$ and using the <Heisenberg uncertainty relation> estimate $r_{\rm typ}\sim\hbar/p_{\rm typ}$ gives
$$
\boxed{r_{\rm typ}\sim\frac{\hbar}{\sqrt{2m_e(54.4\,\mathrm{eV})}}
=2.65\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
The <Bohr model> gives $r_1=a_0/Z=a_0/2=2.65\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm m$ for a heavy helium nucleus, agreeing with the characteristic scale. Equivalently, minimizing the localization estimate $E(r)\sim\hbar^2/(2m_er^2)-Ze^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)$ gives that same scale.

This is an order-of-magnitude radius estimate, not an exact deduction of the cloud's <expected value> from the inequality alone. Numerical coefficients depend on which spread is used; $\Delta x\Delta p\ge\hbar/2$ does not assert $\langle r\rangle=\hbar/p$. For the exact hydrogenic $1s$ state, $\langle r\rangle=3a_0/(2Z)=3.97\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm m$ at $Z=2$, one and a half times the Bohr orbital radius. An electron cloud has a radial distribution, whereas the <Bohr model> assigns a definite radius.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The one-dimensional <time-independent Schrödinger equation> is
$$
\boxed{-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\frac{d^2\psi}{dx^2}+V(x)\psi(x)=E\psi(x).}
$$
The differential term is the <kinetic energy> operator acting on the <wave function>, $V(x)$ supplies the multiplicative <potential energy>, and their sum is the <Hamiltonian operator>. The eigenvalue $E$ is the stationary state's total <energy>. This form assumes a nonrelativistic particle, a time-independent scalar potential, and no additional internal degrees of freedom in the stated model.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The <wave function> is a <probability> amplitude: by the <Born rule>, $|\psi(x)|^2dx$ is the <probability> of a position measurement in an interval of width $dx$. For a normalized bound state $\int|\psi|^2dx=1$, the <expected values> are
$$
\boxed{\langle x\rangle=\int x|\psi(x)|^2\,dx,\qquad
\langle x^2\rangle=\int x^2|\psi(x)|^2\,dx.}
$$
The integrals run over the allowed domain. If the given state is not normalized, divide both by $\int|\psi|^2dx$. Its <variance> is $\langle x^2\rangle-\langle x\rangle^2$; neither $\psi$ itself nor its overall phase is a directly measured position <probability>.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Each <energy eigenstate> acquires its own phase under the time-independent <Hamiltonian operator>:
$$
|\psi(t)\rangle=\frac{e^{-iE_1t/\hbar}|1\rangle+e^{-iE_2t/\hbar}|2\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
$$
Orthonormality removes all off-diagonal terms from the <expected value> of the <energy>, giving
$$
\boxed{\langle E\rangle=\frac{E_1+E_2}{2},\quad\text{independent of time}.}
$$
Return up to an overall phase requires the relative phase to return to one. For $E_1\ne E_2$, the shortest positive recurrence time is
$$
\boxed{\tau=\frac{2\pi\hbar}{|E_2-E_1|}.}
$$
If the two energies coincide, the whole superposition is stationary up to a common phase for every time; there is then no smallest strictly positive recurrence time. A half-period changes the relative sign and produces the orthogonal antisymmetric state rather than the original symmetric state.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Projection onto the antisymmetric <quantum superposition> gives amplitude $\tfrac12(e^{-iE_1t/\hbar}-e^{-iE_2t/\hbar})$. Its squared modulus is
$$
\boxed{P_{+\to-}(t)=\sin^2\left(\frac{(E_2-E_1)t}{2\hbar}\right).}
$$
It vanishes initially, reaches one at a half-recurrence time, and returns to zero after $\tau$. This interference depends on a relative phase even though each energy-eigenstate population and the mean <energy> remain constant.

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

An ideal projective measurement yielding the antisymmetric state prepares that state at $t_1$. Starting from it, the <probability> of reaching the symmetric state after an additional interval $s$ is again $\sin^2((E_2-E_1)s/(2\hbar))$. At the full recurrence interval found above,
$$
\boxed{P\bigl(+\text{ at }t_1+\tau\mid-\text{ at }t_1\bigr)=\sin^2\pi=0.}
$$
The full-period propagator is proportional to the identity on the two-dimensional subspace, so it returns the antisymmetric state to itself, not to its orthogonal partner. The result assumes the stated time-independent evolution continues after the measurement.

==== 6
{scope}

===== Solution

Choose the infinite well as $0<x<a$ with real eigenfunctions $\psi_n=\sqrt{2/a}\sin(n\pi x/a)$. This fixes the relative phase convention for the specified initial plus state. Each of the first two states has <probability> $1/2$ in the left half. Their cross integral is
$$
\int_0^{a/2}\psi_1\psi_2\,dx=\frac2\pi\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin u\sin2u\,du=\frac4{3\pi}.
$$
The evolving superposition therefore gives
$$
\boxed{P_{\rm left}(t)=\frac12+\frac4{3\pi}\cos\left(\frac{(E_2-E_1)t}{\hbar}\right).}
$$
Since $E_n=n^2\pi^2\hbar^2/(2ma^2)$, the angular frequency and ordinary frequency are
$$
\boxed{\omega=\frac{3\pi^2\hbar}{2ma^2},\qquad
f=\frac{\omega}{2\pi}=\frac{3h}{8ma^2}.}
$$
The <probability> varies between $1/2\pm4/(3\pi)$ and remains in $[0,1]$. Changing the sign convention of one eigenfunction while keeping the same physical initial state requires changing its superposition coefficient too; otherwise one has prepared the other initial state and reversed the initial spatial bias.

\Image[2012-s3-two-state-probabilities.png]
{title=Original two-state transition and left-half well <probabilities> over two recurrence periods}

== S4
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Energy studies}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Annual conversion is reduced by several multiplicative losses. Only roughly $40$–$50\%$ of incident sunlight lies in the photosynthetically useful visible band. Reflection and transmission prevent complete absorption; the photon budget and unavoidable relaxation losses limit conversion of absorbed radiation into chemical energy. <Photosynthesis> also has to support respiration and maintenance, while seasonal growth, incomplete canopy cover, water and nutrient limitations reduce the annual harvested yield.

An illustrative loss budget, not a universal biological constant, is $0.45\times0.8\times0.25\times0.5\times0.11\simeq0.005$. The factors represent useful spectrum, absorption, photochemical conversion, retained net production, and combined annual interception and growing-condition limitations, respectively. Thus $\boxed{\eta_{\rm annual}\sim0.5\%}$ is plausible for a land-averaged crop yield even though a leaf under favourable illumination can be more efficient. The result depends on crop, climate, harvested fraction and the definition of dry <biomass energy>. When the input is already annual mean insolation, night-time must not be counted as a second independent loss.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Take an explicitly illustrative European annual mean ground-level irradiance of $100\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, the stated conversion fraction $0.005$, and dry carbohydrate heating value $16\,\mathrm{MJ\,kg^{-1}}$. One square kilometre then produces
$$
M=\frac{(100)(10^6)(3.156\times10^7)(0.005)}{16\times10^6}
=9.86\times10^5\,\mathrm{kg\,year^{-1}}.
$$
Hence $\boxed{M\sim10^3\,\mathrm{tonnes\,km^{-2}\,year^{-1}}}$, or about $10\,\mathrm{tonnes\,ha^{-1}\,year^{-1}}$, as a dry-mass order-of-magnitude estimate. The assumed irradiance is not a site-specific value supplied by the paper. The result scales linearly with irradiance and conversion efficiency and inversely with heating value; wet harvested mass would additionally depend on water content.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Use equal combustion energy as the displacement criterion. The petrol mass and energy are
$$
M_p=0.73(10^{10})=7.3\times10^9\,\mathrm{kg},\qquad
Q_p=M_p(43.5\times10^6)=3.1755\times10^{17}\,\mathrm J.
$$
This needs ethanol mass $M_e=Q_p/(30.5\times10^6)=1.04115\times10^{10}\,\mathrm{kg}$. The <ethanol fermentation> stoichiometry converts $180$ mass units of glucose to $92$ of ethanol, so the ethanol yield per square kilometre is $(1500)(92/180)=766.667$ tonnes. Therefore
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{1.04115\times10^7}{766.667}\simeq1.358\times10^4\,\mathrm{km^2}.}
$$
The carbon mass fraction in octane is $8(12)/(8(12)+18)=96/114$. The displaced fossil carbon is thus
$$
\boxed{\Delta M_{C,\rm fossil}=7.3\times10^9\frac{96}{114}\frac1{1000}
=6.15\times10^6\,\mathrm{tonnes\ C\,year^{-1}}.}
$$
This is the ideal net reduction if the crop regrows and its <carbon dioxide> uptake balances the biogenic carbon released in fermentation and combustion, with negligible additional life-cycle emissions. Ethanol combustion is not carbon-free: it releases about $5.43$ million tonnes of carbon here, and fermentation releases further biogenic carbon. Those are balanced by crop uptake in the stated ideal accounting, rather than being ignored. Farming, fertilizer manufacture, processing heat, transport and land-use carbon losses reduce the actual benefit and may even reverse it. The equal-heat calculation also assumes equal useful-energy conversion efficiencies in the displaced application; it does not establish equal engine mileage automatically.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Three advantages of <biomass energy> are:

* Sustainable regrowth can replenish the fuel and recycle its biogenic carbon, unlike depletion of fossil carbon stocks.
* Chemical fuel can be stored and transported, permitting dispatchable heat, electricity or liquid-fuel production rather than requiring use at the instant of sunlight capture.
* Suitable agricultural residues and organic wastes can supply useful energy while recovering value from material that would otherwise need disposal.

Three disadvantages are:

* Dedicated energy crops compete for land and water, potentially displacing food production and damaging biodiversity.
* Low annual areal productivity, bulky feedstock and moisture increase collection, transport, drying and conversion costs.
* Combustion produces local air pollutants, and processing emissions or land-use carbon debt can undermine the claimed climate saving. A <life-cycle assessment> and a regrowth timescale are necessary; “renewable” alone does not prove low net emissions.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

An ideal <Rankine cycle> has four main steps: liquid water is pumped reversibly to high pressure; heat is added at high pressure to produce hot vapour or supercritical working fluid; the fluid expands approximately isentropically through a turbine to produce work; and low-pressure heat rejection in a condenser returns it to liquid. For steady flow, the main work and heat transfers are differences of <specific enthalpy>.

A steam implementation of the <Carnot cycle> would require compression of a wet liquid-vapour mixture and reversible isothermal heat transfer, both difficult to achieve with practical machinery. Complete condensation before the Rankine pump makes compression work small because liquid specific volume is small. High-pressure heating and superheating can then occur in a boiler without demanding isothermal addition of all the heat. Superheating also reduces turbine-exit moisture, although the calculated exit may still be wet. The pump, turbine, condenser and boiler can operate continuously as separate flow devices. The <Rankine cycle> sacrifices some ideal <thermal efficiency> in return for a practicable steam-plant configuration; no real engine can exceed the <Carnot efficiency> for the same extreme reservoir temperatures.

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume an ideal isentropic turbine and use the supplied table consistently. Its inlet <specific entropy> is $s_3=6.282\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. At the condenser pressure the outlet mixture has $s_4=s_f+\chi(s_g-s_f)$, where $\chi$ is the vapour mass fraction. Hence
$$
\boxed{\chi=\frac{6.282-0.297}{8.667-0.297}=0.71505.}
$$
The outlet is approximately $71.5\%$ vapour and $28.5\%$ liquid by mass. Its <specific enthalpy> is
$$
h_4=84+0.71505(2538-84)=1838.74\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}.
$$
The source's common unit label omits the kelvin denominator for entropy: $s$ must be interpreted in $\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, while $h$ is in $\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}$. A real irreversible turbine would increase entropy and require an efficiency correction; no such correction is supplied.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

For the liquid pump take the explicit incompressible-water estimate $v_f\simeq10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}}$. Its work input is
$$
w_p\simeq v_f(P_2-P_1)=10^{-3}(28-0.002)10^6=27.998\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}},
$$
so $h_2=h_1+w_p=111.998\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}$. With $h_3=3463$ and the preceding $h_4=1838.74$ in the same units,
$$
q_{\rm in}=h_3-h_2=3351.002,\qquad
w_{\rm net}=h_3-h_4-w_p=1596.26.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\eta=\frac{w_{\rm net}}{q_{\rm in}}=0.47635\simeq47.6\%.}
$$
If pump work is neglected because no liquid density was tabulated, the corresponding approximation is $(3463-1838.74)/(3463-84)=48.1\%$. Stating the approximation explains the small difference. The temperature-extreme <Carnot efficiency> is about $1-293.15/873.15=66.4\%$, above both results; finite-temperature-range heat addition in the <Rankine cycle> does not attain that limit.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Post-combustion <carbon capture and storage> separates <carbon dioxide> from an already formed flue gas, often by absorption in a regenerable solvent. The recovered gas must be purified, dehydrated, compressed, transported and injected into a suitable geological store. Caprock integrity, trapping mechanisms, monitoring and long-term liability matter as much as capture at the chimney.

Dilute <carbon dioxide> mixed with nitrogen requires a large gas-processing plant. Solvent regeneration consumes heat; compression consumes power; impurities can cause solvent degradation and corrosion. Retrofitting adds equipment, cooling demand and cost, and lowers net electrical output per unit fuel. Storage capacity, injection performance, leakage risk and public acceptance must be demonstrated, while upstream fuel-cycle emissions remain.

A capture fraction is not the same as the reduction per delivered kilowatt-hour. For illustrative capture $\chi=0.9$ and a plant-efficiency decrease from $0.4$ to $0.3$, residual stack emissions per delivered unit scale as $(1-\chi)(0.4/0.3)=0.133$, corresponding to an $86.7\%$ reduction rather than $90\%$. A full <life-cycle assessment> includes the additional fuel and upstream emissions. These figures illustrate the accounting and are not claimed as universal plant performance.

=== 3
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

Variability reduces firm capacity and creates balancing costs, but does not impose a universal tiny upper limit on the energy fraction from <wind power>. As an illustrative scale calculation, $10\,\mathrm{GW}$ installed at <capacity factor> $0.30$ produces $26.3\,\mathrm{TWh}$ annually, about $8.8\%$ of an assumed $300\,\mathrm{TWh}$ annual demand. Larger installations can supply larger fractions if the rest of the system is adapted.

Geographical spread, forecasting, interconnection, flexible generation, demand response and <energy storage> manage fluctuations on different timescales. They are not interchangeable: a battery that balances seconds does not necessarily cover a week of low wind. Correlated weather, curtailment, network constraints and reserve requirements become more important at high penetration. Backup capacity need not burn fuel continuously at the wind plant's nameplate output; energy and capacity should be accounted for separately. The defensible conclusion is that significant penetration requires system planning and has costs, not that variability alone confines wind to a negligible contribution.<IPCC Special Report on Renewable Energy Sources and Climate Change Mitigation (2011)>[Chapter 8, integration of renewable sources through networks, balancing and flexible energy systems]{ref}

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

The judgement depends on the objectives and the replacement system, rather than following from a single technological fact. In the 2012 context, Germany had decided after Fukushima to accelerate its planned nuclear exit, with a timetable then extending to 2022. The contemporary official statement presented this as a reassessment of residual risk and a transition to efficiency and renewable supply.<German Federal Foreign Office statement on the energy transition (16 February 2012)>[Sections on the 2011 nuclear decision and the proposed replacement energy strategy]{ref}

There is a serious climate opportunity cost if low-operational-carbon nuclear generation is replaced by unabated fossil generation. For example, replacing an illustrative $100\,\mathrm{TWh\,year^{-1}}$ with generation emitting $400$ or $900\,\mathrm{g\,CO_2\,kWh^{-1}}$ adds $40$ or $90$ million tonnes of <carbon dioxide> annually. Replacing that energy by wind at <capacity factor> $0.30$ would require about $38\,\mathrm{GW}$ of nameplate capacity, plus an adequate reliability plan; equal annual energy is not equal firm capacity.

Against continued operation must be weighed accident consequences, waste management, ageing, public preferences and the costs and feasibility of alternatives. Germany's specific risk assessment cannot be inferred solely from the different geography of Fukushima. A fast exit without credible low-carbon replacement can conflict with emissions goals; an exit supported by efficiency, infrastructure and low-carbon alternatives can pursue other legitimate objectives. Calling the decision intrinsically rational or irrational without those comparisons is not a technical conclusion. This answer evaluates the choice as posed in 2012, rather than importing later events as foreknowledge.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

<Tidal power> offers predictable astronomical timing, while <wave power> can exploit an energetic marine resource. Neither is constant: tidal streams have slack-water periods, and wave energy varies with weather and season. Diversity can help a renewable portfolio, but resource size does not by itself establish economical extractable output.

For scale, a mean marine output of $5\,\mathrm{GW}$ would produce $43.8\,\mathrm{TWh}$ per year, about $15\%$ of an assumed $300\,\mathrm{TWh}$ annual electricity demand. Achieving that mean at <capacity factor> $0.30$ would require about $17\,\mathrm{GW}$ installed. This is an illustrative deployment requirement, not a measured UK resource estimate or a 2012 forecast. Tidal-stream kinetic power density scales as $\rho v^3/2$, making energetic sites valuable, but channel-wide extraction also changes the flow, so isolated-rotor power densities cannot simply be multiplied by sea area.

Harsh marine conditions, access for maintenance, survivability in storms, cable connections, navigation and ecological effects constrain deployment. Barrages additionally change estuaries and sediment transport. Research and demonstration may be worthwhile for energy diversity and industrial capability, but “important to investigate” is weaker than “indispensable at any cost”. Marine investment should be compared with efficiency and other low-carbon options using delivered energy, system value and environmental impacts.

==== d
{scope}

===== Solution

The MENA solar resource could provide a useful supplement, but geographical potential does not make imports logically essential to European decarbonisation. With an illustrative annual mean insolation $200\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, photovoltaic conversion $15\%$ and system factor $0.8$, mean delivered-at-plant power is $24\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. A collector area of $10^4\,\mathrm{km^2}$ would then give $240\,\mathrm{GW}$ mean or about $2100\,\mathrm{TWh}$ annually before export losses. This is a scale calculation with stipulated assumptions, not a land-availability survey.

Long transmission corridors, converter losses, night-time supply, storage, dust, cooling or cleaning water, financing and political dependence must also be included. Solar thermal storage can shift some output but does not remove every seasonal or geopolitical risk. Projects should benefit host populations and avoid treating nominal desert area as empty infrastructure-ready land.

Europe also has domestic <wind power>, <solar power>, hydroelectricity, potential nuclear generation, demand reduction and other mitigation options. A technically credible combination of those is a counterexample to the word “essential”. MENA imports could reduce costs or improve diversity within a broader low-carbon system; whether they are preferable requires a complete system comparison rather than solar resource density alone.

==== e
{scope}

===== Solution

A common minimum <carbon price> can make the external cost of emissions visible in investment and dispatch decisions, encourage reductions where they are cheapest, and reduce incentives to relocate emissions-intensive production to unpriced jurisdictions. A credible rising or durable price signal can be more useful for long-lived investment than a volatile short-term spot price.

For an illustrative price of $40$ currency units per tonne of <carbon dioxide>, generation emitting $0.9$ tonnes per MWh incurs $36$ units per MWh, while generation at $0.4$ tonnes per MWh incurs $16$. Those changes are large enough to affect some operating choices, but the actual switching point depends on fuel prices, efficiency and capacity constraints.

A price is not sufficient by itself. Measurement, enforcement, distributional effects and revenue use matter; infrastructure, innovation spillovers, information failures and landlord-tenant incentives can justify complementary policies. Nor is a uniform international minimum a logical prerequisite for any effective reduction: standards, clean-power deployment and efficiency policies can reduce emissions even before every country agrees. The strongest defensible claim is that broad, credible pricing is a valuable coordination instrument, not a uniquely necessary or automatically sufficient solution.<IPCC Special Report on Renewable Energy Sources and Climate Change Mitigation (2011)>[Chapter 11, policy, financing and implementation: instruments operate within wider institutional and market conditions]{ref}

==== f
{scope}

===== Solution

A <plug-in hybrid electric vehicle> can move short journeys to electricity while retaining liquid-fuel range for longer ones. It can reduce oil dependence and some local tailpipe emissions, but the carbon benefit depends on charging, trip length, electricity supply, vehicle manufacture and the fuel used when the engine runs.

For illustration, electricity consumption $0.20\,\mathrm{kWh\,km^{-1}}$ at grid intensity $500\,\mathrm{g\,CO_2\,kWh^{-1}}$ gives $100\,\mathrm{g\,CO_2\,km^{-1}}$ in use. A petrol vehicle using $0.07$ litres per km emits about $158\,\mathrm{g\,CO_2\,km^{-1}}$ from combustion under the paper's octane-density assumptions. Ignoring other life-cycle differences, their use-phase break-even electricity intensity is therefore roughly $790\,\mathrm{g\,CO_2\,kWh^{-1}}$. Cleaner electricity improves the electric case; sufficiently carbon-intensive electricity can reverse it. These are conditional comparisons, not measured fleet averages.

A plug-in hybrid that is rarely charged may obtain little benefit while carrying both propulsion systems. Battery and fuel production also belong in a <life-cycle assessment>. Fully electric vehicles, efficient conventional vehicles, public transport, walking, cycling and reduced travel demand provide alternative or complementary routes. Plug-in hybrids can be a useful transition technology, but no physical argument makes them an essential component of every successful climate strategy.

== S7
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Classical mechanics}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Hamilton's principle> states that the physical path makes the <action> $S=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}L(\mathbf q,\dot{\mathbf q},t)\,dt$ stationary under differentiable variations $\delta q_i$ vanishing at the fixed endpoints. Its conventional “least action” name does not guarantee a minimum. Expanding to first order and integrating the velocity term by parts gives
$$
\delta S=\left[\sum_i\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\delta q_i\right]_{t_i}^{t_f}
+\int_{t_i}^{t_f}\sum_i\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}-\frac d{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\right)\delta q_i\,dt.
$$
The endpoint term vanishes. By the <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations>, the coefficients of the independent interior variations must vanish, yielding
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}-\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}=0.}
$$
For example, the zero path of a harmonic oscillator with endpoints zero can have both positive and negative second variations when the elapsed time exceeds a half-period; stationary action then need not be a least value. The Euler-Lagrange condition is the local statement that the variational principle actually supplies.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Euler-Lagrange equations> in cylindrical coordinates are
$$
\boxed{\ddot R=R\dot\phi^2-\frac{C\dot z}{R},\qquad
\frac d{dt}(R^2\dot\phi)=0,\qquad
\ddot z=\frac{C\dot R}{R}.}
$$
The last two follow because $\phi$ and $z$ are <cyclic coordinates>. They give the <conserved quantities>
$$
\boxed{\ell=R^2\dot\phi,\qquad k=\dot z-C\log R.}
$$
Since the <Lagrangian> is autonomous, its energy function is conserved as well:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal E=\dot R\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot R}
+\dot\phi\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot\phi}
+\dot z\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot z}-L
=\frac12(\dot R^2+R^2\dot\phi^2+\dot z^2).}
$$
The velocity-linear term cancels from this energy, but it still changes the equations of motion. A dimensionless logarithm means $\log(R/R_0)$ for some fixed reference length; replacing the printed $\log R$ by that expression changes the <Lagrangian> only by a total time derivative and shifts the numerical value of $k$, not the trajectory.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Write $w=a^2\Omega^2/C$, requiring $C\ne0$ and $a>0$. The initial conditions fix $\ell=a^2\Omega$ and $\dot z=w+C\log(R/a)$. At $R=a$, the radial force is $a\Omega^2-Cw/a=0$. Thus the unperturbed motion is the helix
$$
\boxed{R=a,\qquad\phi=\Omega t,\qquad z=wt.}
$$
To analyse a small initial radial velocity, eliminate the cyclic velocities using the conserved quantities. The radial energy equation is $\dot R^2/2+U(R)=\mathcal E$, with
$$
U(R)=\frac{a^4\Omega^2}{2R^2}+\frac12\left[w+C\log(R/a)\right]^2.
$$
Here $U'(a)=0$ and $U''(a)=2\Omega^2+C^2/a^2>0$. The circular cylindrical radius is therefore stable. For $\eta=R-a$, the first-order equation is $\ddot\eta+\omega_r^2\eta=0$, so
$$
\boxed{\omega_r^2=2\Omega^2+\frac{C^2}{a^2},\qquad
R=a+\frac{\delta}{\omega_r}\sin\omega_rt+O(\delta^2).}
$$
The orbit acquires small radial oscillations. The other coordinates are modulated consistently with the same constants:
$$
\phi=\Omega t-\frac{2\Omega\delta}{a\omega_r^2}(1-\cos\omega_rt)+O(\delta^2),\qquad
z=wt+\frac{C\delta}{a\omega_r^2}(1-\cos\omega_rt)+O(\delta^2).
$$
These expressions retain the stated initial angular and axial velocities. The perturbation adds $\delta^2/2$ to the conserved energy. The expansion assumes $|\delta|/(a\omega_r)\ll1$, which follows from the supplied smallness condition when $\Omega\ne0$; it is not an exact finite-amplitude helix.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

It can describe the motion per unit mass of a charged particle in the azimuthal <magnetic field> of a long straight current-carrying wire. Choose magnetic <vector potential>
$$
\mathbf A=-\frac{mC}{q}\log(R/R_0)\,\hat{\mathbf z}.
$$
Then $\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A=(mC/(qR))\hat{\boldsymbol\phi}$ and $(T+q\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf v)/m$ is the given <Lagrangian>. In terms of a straight-wire current $I$, $B_\phi=\mu_0I/(2\pi R)$, so $C=q\mu_0I/(2\pi m)$ with signs fixed by charge and current direction.

A static magnetic force does no work, consistent with the conserved <kinetic energy>. Its radial component is $-q\dot zB_\phi$ and its axial component is $q\dot RB_\phi$, reproducing the radial and axial couplings above. The reference length in the logarithm is a gauge convention, not a new physical wire radius in the ideal exterior-field model.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

For the canonical <Poisson bracket>, direct substitution of $F=q_i$ and $F=p_i$ gives
$$
\{q_i,G\}=\frac{\partial G}{\partial p_i},\qquad
\{p_i,G\}=-\frac{\partial G}{\partial q_i}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\sum_i\frac{\partial F}{\partial q_i}\{q_i,G\}
+\sum_i\frac{\partial F}{\partial p_i}\{p_i,G\}
=\sum_i\left(F_{q_i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q_i}\right)
=\boxed{\{F,G\}.}
$$
This expresses the bracket as a first-order derivative acting on $F$, with coefficients determined by $G$; it is the phase-space chain-rule form of the definition.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

<Hamilton's equations> are $\dot q_i=H_{p_i}$ and $\dot p_i=-H_{q_i}$. Using the elementary brackets from the previous part gives
$$
\boxed{\dot q_i=\{q_i,H\},\qquad\dot p_i=\{p_i,H\}.}
$$
A <constant of motion> is a function that stays unchanged along each physical trajectory. For a function $C(\mathbf q,\mathbf p)$ with no explicit time dependence, the chain rule and Hamilton's equations give
$$
\frac{dC}{dt}=\sum_i(C_{q_i}\dot q_i+C_{p_i}\dot p_i)
=\{C,H\}.
$$
Thus $\boxed{C\text{ is conserved}\iff\{C,H\}=0}$ on the accessible phase-space domain. If a function explicitly depends on time, the criterion is instead $\partial_tC+\{C,H\}=0$; the simpler statement uses the source's time-independent $C$ assumption.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Put $\ell=p_\phi$. The <Hamiltonian> has no explicit time or azimuthal dependence, so $\dot H=\{H,H\}=0$ and $\dot\ell=-H_\phi=0$. Its equations of motion are
$$
\dot R=p_R,\qquad\dot\phi=\frac\ell{R^2},\qquad
\dot p_R=\frac{\ell^2}{R^3}+\frac A{R^2},\qquad\dot\ell=0.
$$
Write $C=p_R\ell\sin\phi+(\ell^2/R+A)\cos\phi$. Differentiating along these equations gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\dot C={}&\left(\frac{\ell^3}{R^3}+\frac{A\ell}{R^2}\right)\sin\phi
+\frac{p_R\ell^2}{R^2}\cos\phi
-\frac{p_R\ell^2}{R^2}\cos\phi\\
&-\left(\frac{\ell^3}{R^3}+\frac{A\ell}{R^2}\right)\sin\phi=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Equivalently, the same cancellation evaluates $\{C,H\}$. Hence $\boxed{H,\ p_\phi,\ C\text{ are all constants of motion}.}$ The extra conserved component is associated with the Runge-Lenz symmetry of this inverse-distance central potential, rather than with conservation of radial momentum.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Jacobi identity> and the two brackets $\{C,H\}=\{p_\phi,H\}=0$ imply
$$
\{\{C,p_\phi\},H\}
=\{\{C,H\},p_\phi\}+\{C,\{p_\phi,H\}\}=0.
$$
Thus $\boxed{D=\{C,p_\phi\}\text{ is conserved}}$ without re-solving the motion. Explicitly $D=\partial_\phi C=p_Rp_\phi\cos\phi-(p_\phi^2/R+A)\sin\phi$. Squaring and adding the two rotated components gives
$$
\boxed{C^2+D^2=A^2+2Hp_\phi^2.}
$$
Consequently the four quantities are not functionally independent: generically there are **three** independent constants. One may choose, locally, $H,p_\phi$ and the orientation encoded by $C,D$; their magnitude is fixed by the displayed relation. This also matches the maximum of three independent trajectory-labeling constants for a nonstationary autonomous flow in four-dimensional phase space. On singular subsets the local functional rank can be lower: for example, at $A=0$ and $p_\phi=0$, the differentials of $C,D$ are proportional to $dp_\phi$, leaving at most two independent differentials together with $dH$; “three” is the generic count, not an assertion about every singular configuration.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $p_i=\partial L/\partial\dot q_i$ and consider the infinitesimal fixed-time transformation $\delta q_i=\epsilon K_i(\mathbf q)$. It induces $\delta\dot q_i=\epsilon\,dK_i/dt$. Expanding the <Lagrangian> gives
$$
\frac{\delta L}{\epsilon}=\sum_i\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}K_i+p_i\frac{dK_i}{dt}\right).
$$
On a solution of the <Euler-Lagrange equations>, $\partial L/\partial q_i=\dot p_i$, so this equals $d(\sum_ip_iK_i)/dt$. Exact invariance makes the left side zero, proving the fixed-time form of <Noether's theorem>:
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\left(\sum_ip_iK_i\right)=0.}
$$
The symmetry direction need not coincide with one original coordinate axis; $\mathbf K$ can depend on the generalized coordinates. The conclusion is evaluated on physical trajectories, while the assumed invariance is a property of the <Lagrangian> under the transformation.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The canonical momentum for the stated symmetric-gauge <Lagrangian> is
$$
\mathbf p=m\dot{\mathbf x}+\frac q2\mathbf B\times\mathbf x,
\qquad
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\mathbf x}=\frac q2\dot{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf B.
$$
Since $\mathbf B$ is uniform and time independent, its <Euler-Lagrange equation> becomes
$$
m\ddot{\mathbf x}+\frac q2\mathbf B\times\dot{\mathbf x}
-\frac q2\dot{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf B=0,
\qquad\boxed{m\ddot{\mathbf x}=q\dot{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf B.}
$$
This is the magnetic <Lorentz force>, which is unchanged by constant spatial translations. The <Lagrangian> itself changes by
$$
\delta L=\epsilon\frac q2\dot{\mathbf x}\cdot(\mathbf B\times\hat{\mathbf n})
=\epsilon\frac{dF_{\mathbf n}}{dt},\qquad
\boxed{F_{\mathbf n}=\frac q2\mathbf x\cdot(\mathbf B\times\hat{\mathbf n}).}
$$
A total time derivative changes the action only by $\epsilon[F_{\mathbf n}(t_f)-F_{\mathbf n}(t_i)]$. For variations with fixed endpoints this boundary contribution has zero variation, so the equations of motion are unchanged. The translation is therefore a symmetry of the dynamics even though this particular gauge makes the <Lagrangian> only quasi-invariant.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Repeat the expansion in the strict-invariance proof, but now use $\delta L/\epsilon=dF/dt$. Along Euler-Lagrange trajectories,
$$
\frac d{dt}\left(\sum_ip_iK_i\right)=\frac{dF}{dt}.
$$
It follows that the conserved Noether quantity is
$$
\boxed{Q=\sum_ip_iK_i-F.}
$$
The minus sign matters. It subtracts the boundary term created by the symmetry transformation. More explicitly, before using the equations of motion one has
$$
\frac{\delta L}{\epsilon}
=\frac d{dt}(\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf K)
+\sum_i\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}-\dot p_i\right)K_i.
$$
The final sum vanishes on shell, proving the result for arbitrary coordinate-dependent $\mathbf K(\mathbf q)$ under the source's fixed-time assumptions. It is this boundary-term form, rather than the uncorrected canonical momentum alone, that applies to translations in the magnetic example.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

For translation in a fixed direction $\hat{\mathbf n}$, take $\mathbf K=\hat{\mathbf n}$ and the preceding $F_{\mathbf n}$. The triple-product identity gives $F_{\mathbf n}=-(q/2)\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot(\mathbf B\times\mathbf x)$. Thus
$$
Q_{\mathbf n}=\mathbf p\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}-F_{\mathbf n}
=\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot\left(m\dot{\mathbf x}+q\mathbf B\times\mathbf x\right).
$$
Choosing the three Cartesian directions gives the three constants
$$
\boxed{\mathbf K_{\rm mag}=m\dot{\mathbf x}+q\mathbf B\times\mathbf x
=\text{constant vector}.}
$$
These are magnetic pseudomomentum components, not the three components of kinetic momentum $m\dot{\mathbf x}$. A direct check is $d\mathbf K_{\rm mag}/dt=q\dot{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf B+q\mathbf B\times\dot{\mathbf x}=0$. In the stated gauge, $\mathbf K_{\rm mag}=\mathbf p+(q/2)\mathbf B\times\mathbf x$, consistent with the Noether boundary correction.

== S9
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Financial physics}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Stylized facts of financial returns> are robust qualitative statistical regularities found across many instruments or datasets, abstracting from their individual details. Examples include heavy return-distribution tails relative to a Gaussian model, weak linear autocorrelation of many liquid-market returns beyond short lags, and persistent clustering of large absolute or squared returns. Some markets also show asymmetric return-volatility responses and partial movement toward a Gaussian aggregate distribution at longer horizons.

These are empirical tendencies with sampling-scale and market qualifications, not exact universal equations. In particular, weak return autocorrelation does not imply independent increments: volatility clustering and higher-order dependence can remain. A useful model should state which regularities it explains and which its assumptions exclude, rather than treating one convenient random-walk model as an observed law.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $S_T$ be the terminal asset price and $K$ a common strike or forward delivery price. A long European call and put have expiry payoffs
$$
\boxed{C_T=(S_T-K)^+,\qquad P_T=(K-S_T)^+.}
$$
The call is zero below $K$ and then rises with slope one; the put falls with slope minus one below $K$ and is zero above it. A long forward instead has $\boxed{F_T^{\rm long}=S_T-K}$ and a short forward has $\boxed{F_T^{\rm short}=K-S_T}$. Both are straight lines crossing zero at $K$, with negative as well as positive outcomes.

The option's exercise right truncates the unfavourable side, whereas the forward is an obligation. These are **payoffs**, not total profits: an option premium and its financing cost have not been subtracted. A newly agreed fair forward may have zero initial value while still having a nonzero future delivery price.

\Image[2012-s9-expiry-payoffs.png]
{title=Original European call and put payoffs compared with long and short forward-contract payoffs}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume a non-dividend-paying tradable asset follows <geometric Brownian motion> $dS=\mu S\,dt+\sigma S\,dW$, with constant volatility $\sigma$ and risk-free rate $r$. Trading is continuous, frictionless and divisible; borrowing and lending occur at the same rate, short selling is allowed, the strategy is self-financing, and there are no jumps or arbitrage opportunities. These are modelling assumptions, not assertions that market returns are exactly Gaussian or that hedging is costless in practice.

For a smooth option price $V(S,t)$, <Itô's formula> gives
$$
dV=\left(V_t+\mu S V_S+\tfrac12\sigma^2S^2V_{SS}\right)dt+\sigma S V_S\,dW.
$$
Replicate it by $\Delta$ shares and a cash account of value $b=V-\Delta S$. Self-financing requires $dV=\Delta\,dS+rb\,dt$. Matching the stochastic term fixes $\Delta=V_S$. Matching the remaining drift and cancelling the physical drift $\mu$ then gives
$$
\boxed{V_t+\frac12\sigma^2S^2V_{SS}+rSV_S-rV=0.}
$$
This is the <Black-Scholes equation>. The cancellation does not assert that the actual expected asset return equals $r$; risk-neutral valuation is a consequence of replication under these assumptions.

For a European call, impose $V(S,T)=(S-K)^+$, with $V(0,t)=0$ and $V(S,t)\sim S-Ke^{-r(T-t)}$ for large $S$. Its writer hedges the short option by holding $\Delta=\partial V/\partial S$ shares and investing or borrowing the residual cash $V-\Delta S$, rebalancing as $S$ and time change. In the constant-parameter model $\Delta=N(d_1)$, where $d_1=[\log(S/K)+(r+\sigma^2/2)(T-t)]/[\sigma\sqrt{T-t}]$ and $N$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution. Continuous rebalancing is an ideal limit: transaction costs, discrete trading, jumps and changing volatility create residual hedge risk. Writing the self-financing gains equation explicitly avoids incorrectly adding an unfinanced $S\,d\Delta$ term to the strategy.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

<Implied volatility> is the volatility parameter that makes a specified option-pricing model reproduce an observed option price. For a call, it solves
$$
\boxed{C_{\rm BS}(S,K,T-t,r;\sigma_{\rm imp})=C_{\rm market}.}
$$
For positive time to expiry and positive asset and strike prices, the Black-Scholes call price increases with $\sigma$, so an interior arbitrage-consistent price gives a unique finite positive value. It is an inverse model parameter, not the sample standard deviation of past returns or a guaranteed forecast of future realised volatility. Different implied volatilities across strikes or maturities form smiles or surfaces, signalling that one constant volatility does not fit all prices simultaneously.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Independent identically distributed increments have the same marginal distribution at every step and factorizing joint distributions across steps. With finite mean $\mu$ and <variance> $s^2$, an additive random walk has mean displacement $n\mu$ and <variance> $ns^2$ after $n$ steps. Zero increment autocovariances follow from independence, but their converse is false. Gaussian increments are an additional assumption, not part of the definition of a random walk.

To investigate a measured price process, first specify the sampling interval and whether the model concerns price differences or log returns. Remove an estimated drift and examine stationarity, increment distributions, autocorrelations and <variance> growth over nonoverlapping horizons. Also test higher-order statistics, absolute or squared-return correlations, conditional distributions and out-of-sample predictability; those can reveal dependence missed by linear autocorrelation. Check whether different time scales and market regimes give compatible results, and account for bid-ask effects and intraday seasonality at short lags. Finite data cannot prove independence or permanent stationarity. Failure to reject a particular test is weaker than establishing that every joint distribution factorizes; the next example makes that distinction explicit.

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

Take the driving signs $a_t=\alpha(t)$ to be independent across times, with $\mathbb Ea_t=0$ and $a_t^2=1$. Temporal independence of the drivers is an assumption needed in addition to their stated equal one-time <probabilities>. For $X_t=\delta y(t)=a_{t-2}a_{t-1}+a_t$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EX_t=\mathbb Ea_{t-2}\,\mathbb Ea_{t-1}+\mathbb Ea_t=0.}
$$
As a useful scale check, $X_t$ takes values $-2,0,2$ with <probabilities> $1/4,1/2,1/4$. Its <variance> is therefore $\boxed{\mathbb EX_t^2=2}$, rather than the unit <variance> of a single driving sign.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Expand $X_tX_{t'}$ into products of the independent mean-zero signs. Each increment is the sum of two monomials, $a_t$ and $a_{t-2}a_{t-1}$. At distinct times, no monomial from one increment has exactly the same set of sign indices as a monomial from the other. Every product therefore contains at least one sign to an odd power, whose average is zero. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X_{t'}X_t]=0\qquad(t'\ne t).}
$$
This argument covers overlapping driver sets at separations one and two, not just the disjoint case at larger separation. At equal times the value is $2$, so the complete autocovariance is $2\delta_{tt'}$. It establishes absence of second-order serial correlation, not independence of returns.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

Expand the three increments into their eight monomial products. Exactly one term has every driving-sign index appearing an even number of times: take $a_{t-2}a_{t-1}$ from $X_t$, $a_{t-1}$ from $X_{t-1}$, and $a_{t-2}$ from $X_{t-2}$. Their product is $a_{t-1}^2a_{t-2}^2=1$. Every other term leaves an unpaired mean-zero sign. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X_tX_{t-1}X_{t-2}]=1.}
$$
If the increments were independent, their three zero means would instead give zero. The nonzero third-order moment is therefore a direct demonstration of nonlinear temporal dependence despite vanishing nonzero-lag autocovariances.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The series is predictable in a statistical sense beyond linear return autocorrelation. For example, the observed past feature $Z_t=X_{t-1}X_{t-2}$ satisfies $\mathbb E[X_tZ_t]=1$ and $\mathbb EZ_t^2=4$, so its least-squares predictor within the family $bZ_t$ is $\boxed{\widehat X_t=Z_t/4}$. This is a predictor based on observed increments, not an assumption that the hidden driving signs are directly visible; it need not equal the full conditional expectation.

A finite bound on ordinary peak-to-trough <financial drawdown> does **not** follow. Choose a long alternating sequence of driving signs, $a_t=(-1)^t$. It produces $X_t=-1+(-1)^t$, alternating $-2$ and $0$. The price never recovers during that episode and its cumulative loss grows without bound as the episode length grows. Every finite such driving sequence has positive <probability>. Thus $\boxed{\text{no finite worst drawdown magnitude or duration exists without a horizon bound}.}$

More precisely, over an $N$-month window and over admissible starting histories, the maximum possible drawdown is
$$
\boxed{D_{\max}(N)=2\lfloor N/2\rfloor+2\quad(N\ge1).}
$$
One derivation keeps the last two signs as a four-state dynamic program. Let $d_n(a,b)$ be the smallest cumulative increment after $n$ steps ending with signs $(a,b)$, with $d_0(a,b)=0$ for each permitted initial pair. Then $d_{n+1}(b,c)=\min_{a=\pm1}[d_n(a,b)+ab+c]$. Induction gives the negative of the displayed minimum. The bound is attained by increments $(-2,0)^k,-2$ for $N=2k+1$, or $(-2,0)^{k-1},-2,-2$ for $N=2k$, so it is also an attained peak-to-trough loss over $N$ months, not only an algebraic net displacement bound.

For comparison, the index with increments $a_t=\pm1$ has $D_{\max}(N)=N$, attained by $N$ successive negative signs; it too has no finite horizon-free bound. If “drawdown” were intended to mean a **strictly decreasing consecutive run**, the first model permits at most two successive $-2$ increments, for a loss of four over two months, whereas the simple walk permits arbitrarily long such runs. That different run statistic must not be substituted for the usual drawdown definition: zero increments can separate continuing losses without any recovery.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Derivatives can transfer risk, improve price discovery and permit hedging, so their existence is not by itself evidence that they cause crashes. They can also amplify stress through leverage, margin calls, counterparty exposure and forced dynamic hedging into falling markets. A short-gamma hedge can require selling more underlying after a fall, reinforcing feedback when liquidity is thin. The causal issue is the combination of positions, funding, market depth and institutional rules; derivatives are neither necessary nor sufficient for a crash, and correlation between trading volume and a crash does not establish the direction of causation.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Financial data show approximate, scale-dependent regularities rather than an exact universal scaling law. Heavy return tails can resemble a power law over an intermediate range, but the fitted exponent, cutoff and range depend on instrument, period and sampling. A straight segment on a log-log plot alone is not enough to establish a power law.

For independent finite-<variance> increments, <variance> grows linearly with horizon and typical fluctuations scale as $\Delta t^{1/2}$. Central-limit aggregation can make longer-horizon returns more Gaussian even when short-horizon returns are heavy-tailed. Real data can depart from a single self-similar distribution because of volatility clustering, changing regimes, asymmetric responses, intraday seasonality and market microstructure. Apparent different scaling of higher moments may reflect intermittent volatility, but moments estimated from rare tails can also be unstable or may not exist in an idealized model.

Useful tests compare rescaled distributions, structure functions and volatility correlations across several scales, with uncertainty estimates and checks for nonstationarity. Scaling is a valuable approximate modelling guide over a documented range, not proof that one exponent describes every asset and horizon or that historical tail behaviour fixes future extreme losses.

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

After three multiplicative steps, $k$ upward moves and $3-k$ downward moves give
$$
S_3(k)=100(1.1)^k(0.9)^{3-k}.
$$
For $k=0,1,2,3$, respectively, the prices are $\boxed{72.9,\ 89.1,\ 108.9,\ 133.1}$. Since the steps are independent, the endpoint <probabilities> are binomial:
$$
\boxed{\Pr(S_3=S_3(k))=\binom3k p^k(1-p)^{3-k}.}
$$
In the displayed ascending-price order they are $(1-p)^3$, $3p(1-p)^2$, $3p^2(1-p)$, and $p^3$. Different paths with the same count of up moves recombine because multiplication by $1.1$ and $0.9$ commutes.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

The one-step expected price multiplier under the stated physical <probability> is $1.1p+0.9(1-p)=0.9+0.2p$. Independence lets the expectation of the product factorize, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_p[S_3]=100(0.9+0.2p)^3.}
$$
The same expression follows by summing the four binomial endpoint <probabilities> times their prices. This is a physical-measure expectation; option replication below uses a separately determined risk-neutral <probability>.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $q$ be the risk-neutral up <probability> and $R=1.04$ the one-step risk-free gross return. Discounted asset price must be a martingale, so
$$
1.1q+0.9(1-q)=1.04,\qquad
\boxed{q=\frac{1.04-0.9}{1.1-0.9}=0.7.}
$$
The no-arbitrage condition $0.9<R<1.1$ is satisfied, so $q$ lies strictly between zero and one. This pricing <probability> need not equal the actual up frequency $p$.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

At strike $K=108$, the European put payoff is $(108-S_3)^+$. In the ascending-price order $72.9,89.1,108.9,133.1$, its values are
$$
\boxed{35.1,\quad18.9,\quad0,\quad0.}
$$
Risk-neutral expectation discounted for three steps gives
$$
\boxed{P_0=\frac{(0.3)^3(35.1)+3(0.7)(0.3)^2(18.9)}{(1.04)^3}
=4.01809.}
$$
The physical <probability> $p$ drops out because the complete two-state tree admits a replicating hedge. The strike is $108$, so the $108.9$ endpoint is out of the money; replacing it by a positive payoff would materially change the price.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

At each time-2 node, replicate one positive put payoff using $\Delta$ shares and a current cash deposit $b$. Its two terminal values must satisfy $\Delta S_u+Rb=P_u$ and $\Delta S_d+Rb=P_d$, with $R=1.04$. Thus
$$
\Delta=\frac{P_u-P_d}{S_u-S_d},\qquad b=\frac{P_u-\Delta S_u}{1.04}.
$$
For the three nodes the hedge is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
S_2&\Delta&b&P_2=\Delta S_2+b\\\hline
121&0&0&0\\
99&-21/22&103.95/1.04=99.95192&5.45192\\
81&-1&108/1.04=103.84615&22.84615
\end{array}}
$$
At $S_2=121$, both child puts expire worthless. At $99$, the child payoffs are $0$ and $18.9$; at $81$, they are $18.9$ and $35.1$. Substituting the hedge holdings into both child states verifies those payoffs exactly.

The writer is short the option and therefore **holds this positive replicating portfolio** to offset the liability. At the lower nodes that means shorting shares and depositing the proceeds together with the option value, rather than buying positive-delta stock. Rebalancing the replicating holdings along the tree is self-financing in the ideal model; the combined short option plus hedge has zero terminal liability in either child state.

== S10
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Medical and health physics}

TODO: the printed index lists this option, but the supplied 29-page source contains no S10 question pages. No question numbering or statements have been inferred.

== S12
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Introduction to biophysics}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Protein primary structure> is the ordered <amino acid> sequence of a <polypeptide>, linked by covalent <peptide bonds>. The approximately planar <peptide bond> restricts local geometry, while backbone torsion angles give conformational freedom subject to steric exclusion. <Protein secondary structure> comprises local backbone arrangements, especially the <alpha helix>, with backbone <hydrogen bonds> between residues $i$ and $i+4$, and the <beta sheet>, whose strands are joined by backbone <hydrogen bonds>. Turns and loops connect these elements. <Protein tertiary structure> is the full three-dimensional organization of a single chain, often including several <protein domains>. <Protein quaternary structure> is the arrangement of multiple chains in a <protein complex>.

<Protein folding> is governed by the <Gibbs free energy> of the chain together with its solvent, not by the chain's <energy> alone. Folding reduces the chain's <conformational entropy>, but can gain solvent <entropy> through the <hydrophobic effect>, burying nonpolar side chains away from water. Favorable <hydrogen bonds>, <van der Waals forces>, electrostatic interactions and sometimes covalent <disulfide bonds> stabilize particular structures. Steric repulsion prevents atomic overlap. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, the native ensemble is favored when its total $\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S$ is lower than competing ensembles. The balance depends on the solvent, <pH>, salt and ligands. <Molecular chaperones> help avoid aggregation or kinetic traps; a stable fold is not a perfectly rigid conformation, and some proteins have disordered regions.

For a soluble globular <protein>, a typical pattern is a nonpolar core and a polar, hydrated surface. A <transmembrane protein> instead presents nonpolar side chains to the hydrocarbon interior of the <lipid bilayer>. Membrane-spanning <alpha helices> satisfy their polar backbone groups through internal <hydrogen bonds>; a roughly membrane-thickness helix commonly contains about twenty residues, depending on tilt and bilayer thickness. Multiple helices can enclose polar channels or binding sites. Membrane-spanning <beta barrels> likewise expose mainly nonpolar groups outward towards lipid while arranging backbone <hydrogen bonds> between strands and often lining an aqueous pore with polar groups. Exposed loops and domains contacting water can resemble soluble <proteins>. Not every membrane-associated <protein> spans the membrane: peripheral attachment and lipid anchoring are distinct possibilities.

**The organizing distinction is the environment of each surface: water favors accessible polar groups, while the membrane core favors outward-facing nonpolar groups.** Buried polar groups can still be essential to a transport pathway or catalytic site.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<ATP synthase> couples downhill proton motion across a <cell membrane> to synthesis of <ATP> from <ADP> and inorganic phosphate. Its membrane-embedded $F_o$ sector contains an $a$ subunit with offset proton-access half-channels and a rotating $c$-subunit ring. Reversible protonation of sites on the ring permits rotation relative to the stationary $a$ subunit. The central rotor transmits this motion through the $\gamma$ shaft to the soluble $F_1$ head. A peripheral stator holds the head relative to the membrane sector. The common bacterial schematic has an $\alpha_3\beta_3$ head, catalytic sites mainly on the three $\beta$ subunits, and a central $\gamma/\epsilon$ rotor; accessory stator components depend on the organism.

The <binding change mechanism> cycles the catalytic sites between open, loose-binding and tight-binding conformations. Rotor orientation changes their affinities for <ADP>, phosphate and <ATP>; the sites operate out of phase. Tight binding favors formation of bound <ATP>, and mechanical rotation changes the conformation to permit product release. In the ideal tightly coupled cycle, one revolution gives three catalytic turnovers:
$$
\boxed{3\ \text{ATP per }360^\circ,\qquad 1\ \text{ATP per }120^\circ.}
$$
The proton count is not universally fixed: an ideal ring with $n_c$ proton-carrying subunits gives $n_c$ protons per revolution and $n_c/3$ protons per synthesized <ATP>, before additional transport costs.

For a proton moving from side 1 to side 2, its molar <electrochemical potential> change is
$$
\Delta\mu_{\mathrm H^+}=RT\log\frac{a_{\mathrm H^+,2}}{a_{\mathrm H^+,1}}+F(\psi_2-\psi_1).
$$
The chemical gradient and membrane <electric potential difference> together supply the <proton-motive force>. Synthesis requires the proton free-energy release to meet the positive <Gibbs free energy> of <ATP> synthesis. The coupling is reversible: isolated $F_1$ supplied with <ATP> can hydrolyse it and rotate its shaft, while the intact enzyme can use hydrolysis-driven rotation to pump protons against their gradient. The sketch is structural and schematic; it does not reconstruct the absent stepping trace.

\Image[2012-s12-atp-synthase-and-dwell-distributions.png]
{title=ATP synthase architecture and the two derived dwell-time distributions}
{description=The structural panel shows a membrane rotor, shaft, stator and three catalytic sites. The distribution panel uses explicitly illustrative dimensionless rates, not values read from the absent source stepping plot.}

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $S_1(t)$ be the probability that an ATP-waiting state has not yet bound a substrate molecule. A constant bimolecular binding coefficient $k_1$ at fixed <ATP> concentration $c$ gives a pseudo-first-order hazard $\lambda=k_1c$. Thus $\dot S_1=-\lambda S_1$, $S_1(0)=1$, and the <waiting-time distribution> is
$$
\boxed{P_1(t)=-\dot S_1(t)=k_1c\,e^{-k_1ct},\quad t\ge0,\qquad \langle t_1\rangle=\frac1{k_1c}.}
$$
The <probability density function> starts at $k_1c$ and decreases exponentially. Increasing $c$ shortens the waiting time in inverse proportion. This supports one rate-limiting ATP-binding event before the associated motion, rather than a concentration-independent chemical delay.

For the second delay, consider sequential independent first-order transitions $A\mathrel{\mathop{\longrightarrow}^{k_a}}B\mathrel{\mathop{\longrightarrow}^{k_b}}C$. Their dwell times have <exponential distributions>. The total waiting time is their sum, so <convolution> gives
$$
P_2(t)=\int_0^t k_ae^{-k_as}k_be^{-k_b(t-s)}\,ds
=\boxed{\frac{k_ak_b}{k_a-k_b}\left(e^{-k_bt}-e^{-k_at}\right),\quad t\ge0.}
$$
It is normalized, symmetric under exchange of $k_a,k_b$, and has
$$
\boxed{\langle t_2\rangle=\frac1{k_a}+\frac1{k_b},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(t_2)=\frac1{k_a^2}+\frac1{k_b^2}.}
$$
Unlike one exponential delay, it vanishes at $t=0$, rises initially as $k_ak_bt$, reaches a maximum at $t_{\rm mode}=\log(k_a/k_b)/(k_a-k_b)$ and eventually decays at the slower rate. For equal rates the continuous limit is $P_2(t)=k^2t e^{-kt}$, an <Erlang distribution> with mode $1/k$. If one rate is much faster than the other, the observed long-time behavior approaches one exponential. The sketch in the preceding section displays these shapes using arbitrary specified rates.

The simplest <catalytic cycle> compatible with the fits therefore contains an ATP-dependent binding delay and at least two resolvable ATP-concentration-independent kinetic events. Hydrolysis, product release and conformational changes are possible microscopic assignments, but the two-rate fit alone does not uniquely identify them or the order of $k_a$ and $k_b$. If each complete $120^\circ$ cycle contains these three waits and step durations are negligible, its mean duration is $(k_1c)^{-1}+k_a^{-1}+k_b^{-1}$ and the mean revolution rate is the inverse of three times this duration. Multiple catalytic sites can cooperate during that cycle.

**TODO: the source PDF's stepping plot is absent. Its individual substep angles, numerical timings and the mapping of plotted dwell labels onto the larger or smaller angular movement cannot be read.** The surviving text establishes only the $120^\circ$ total step and the existence of a larger and a smaller substep. No numerical rate, missing angle or plotted label is inferred from a different experiment. The distribution derivations and the concentration-dependence inference above are independent of that illustration.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Write the one-dimensional <random walk> endpoint as $x_m=\sum_{j=1}^m\xi_j$, starting at the origin. Assume unbiased, independent increments, each with $\langle\xi_j\rangle=0$ and $\langle\xi_j^2\rangle=\Delta$. Expanding the square gives
$$
\langle x_m^2\rangle=\sum_j\langle\xi_j^2\rangle+2\sum_{i<j}\langle\xi_i\xi_j\rangle.
$$
Independence makes each cross expectation $\langle\xi_i\rangle\langle\xi_j\rangle=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\langle x_m\rangle=0,\qquad \langle x_m^2\rangle=m\Delta.}
$$
Uncorrelated increments with the same second moments would suffice. Unbiasedness alone does not remove correlations: persistent or anticorrelated steps need not satisfy this result. The symbol $\Delta$ is already the mean-square step size, with units of length squared.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $C(x)$ be the particle <number density>, $U(x)$ the conservative <potential energy> and $F=-dU/dx$ the applied <force>. In the overdamped regime a viscous <drag coefficient> $\zeta$ gives drift <velocity> $v_d=F/\zeta$. Combining this drift with <Fick's first law> gives the number flux
$$
J=-D\frac{dC}{dx}+\frac F\zeta C.
$$
At <thermal equilibrium>, the <Boltzmann distribution> is $C_{\rm eq}\propto e^{-U/(k_{\rm B}T)}$, so $dC_{\rm eq}/dx=FC_{\rm eq}/(k_{\rm B}T)$. Setting its net flux to zero yields
$$
0=FC_{\rm eq}\left(\frac1\zeta-\frac D{k_{\rm B}T}\right),\qquad
\boxed{D=\frac{k_{\rm B}T}{\zeta}=\mu k_{\rm B}T,\quad\mu=\zeta^{-1}.}
$$
This is the <Einstein diffusion relation>. The derivation assumes a dilute equilibrium suspension, uniform <temperature>, linear drift response and a well-defined long-time <diffusion coefficient>. With a nonzero conservative <force>, the full transport equation includes drift; the force-free equation in the next section describes spreading without that drift.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

For constant $D$ and no drift, particle conservation and <Fick's first law> give the one-dimensional <diffusion equation> $\partial_t C=D\partial_x^2 C$. For $C=A t^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/(4Dt)}$,
$$
\partial_t C=C\left(-\frac1{2t}+\frac{x^2}{4Dt^2}\right),\qquad
\partial_x^2 C=C\left(-\frac1{2Dt}+\frac{x^2}{4D^2t^2}\right),
$$
so direct substitution verifies the equation for $t>0$. The <Gaussian integral> fixes the normalization:
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}C\,dx=A\sqrt{4\pi D}=N,\qquad
\boxed{C(x,t)=\frac{N}{\sqrt{4\pi Dt}}\exp\left(-\frac{x^2}{4Dt}\right).}
$$
This is the <heat kernel> for a point release, with $C(x,0)=N\delta(x)$ in the distributional limit. It remains centered at zero while its width grows as $t^{1/2}$ and its peak falls as $t^{-1/2}$. To compute the second moment, set $y=x/\sqrt{4Dt}$:
$$
\langle x^2\rangle=\frac1N\int x^2C\,dx
=\frac{4Dt}{\sqrt\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}y^2e^{-y^2}\,dy
=\boxed{2Dt},
$$
using $\int y^2e^{-y^2}dy=\sqrt\pi/2$ over the whole real line, obtained by integration by parts.

If the mean number of independent unbiased jumps in time $t$ is $\langle m\rangle=ft$, conditioning on $m$ gives $\langle x^2(t)\rangle=\Delta\langle m\rangle=f\Delta t$. Matching it to the continuum <diffusion> result yields
$$
\boxed{D=\frac{f\Delta}{2}.}
$$
For fixed-magnitude steps $a$, $\Delta=a^2$. The equality of second moments also holds for Poisson jump times independent of the increments; a Gaussian continuum approximation additionally requires many steps and finite step variance.

\Image[2012-s12-diffusion-gaussians.png]
{title=Spreading of a normalized one-dimensional diffusion packet}
{description=Analytic Gaussian profiles with N=1 and D=1 at three illustrative times have fixed unit area, peak proportional to inverse square root of time, and variance 2Dt.}

== S16
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Plasma physics}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Landau damping> is the collisionless decay of a plasma wave through resonant energy exchange with particles whose velocity is close to the wave's <phase velocity> $v_\phi=\omega/k$. Particles slightly slower than the wave tend to gain energy from it; particles slightly faster can give energy to it. For the decreasing resonant part of a <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution>, the net transfer is from the wave to the electrons, so the wave amplitude decays.

In the linear <Vlasov equation> description this is <collisionless phase mixing>, not a requirement for frequent particle collisions. Fine-scale structure develops in the velocity dependence of the perturbation even while the macroscopic electric field decreases. The resonant contribution to the growth rate has the sign of $\partial f/\partial v$ at a positive wave phase velocity: a negative slope produces damping, whereas a sufficiently positive slope can drive an instability. A population comparison must be local to the resonance; merely counting all particles faster and slower than the wave is not the criterion.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Define the one-dimensional thermal widths $\sigma_p=\sqrt{k_{\rm B}T_p/m_e}$ and $\sigma_b=\sqrt{k_{\rm B}T_b/m_e}$. Normalize each <velocity distribution> to its electron number density:
$$
f_p(v)=\frac{n_p}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_p}e^{-v^2/(2\sigma_p^2)},\qquad
f_b(v)=\frac{n_b}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_b}e^{-(v-V)^2/(2\sigma_b^2)}.
$$
The total is $f=f_p+f_b$. It consists of a broad peak centred at zero and a much narrower displaced beam near $V$, with $V\gg\sigma_p\gg\sigma_b$. Their areas, rather than their peak heights, are $n_p$ and $n_b$. A weak narrow beam can make a positive-slope region on its low-velocity side even while contributing little to the total density.

\Image[2012-s16-beam-distribution.png]
{title=Broad plasma Maxwellian and a narrow displaced electron beam}
{description=Illustrative normalized widths and density ratio are used to display the beam on the background tail. The marked V−sigma_b is the point of maximum positive beam slope, not a universal exact marginal-mode velocity.}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

For the shifted beam <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution>,
$$
f_b'(v)=\frac{n_b(V-v)}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_b^3}e^{-(v-V)^2/(2\sigma_b^2)}.
$$
On the rising flank put $u=(V-v)/\sigma_b>0$. The positive slope is proportional to $u e^{-u^2/2}$, whose derivative is $(1-u^2)e^{-u^2/2}$. Its unique positive maximum is at $u=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{v_\phi\simeq V-\sigma_b=V-\sqrt{\frac{k_{\rm B}T_b}{m_e}}.}
$$
This is the region where beam drive can most readily overcome the negative plasma slope when that background changes little over the narrow beam width. It is an estimate for the first resonant unstable wave, not a proof that every beam-plasma dispersion relation has its exact marginal phase velocity at this value. Both the total slope and the real dispersion relation determine the exact marginal mode.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

At $v_*=V-\sigma_b$, the beam's positive slope is $n_b e^{-1/2}/(\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_b^2)$, while the background slope has magnitude $n_pv_*e^{-v_*^2/(2\sigma_p^2)}/(\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma_p^3)$. Thus a nonnegative total slope at this velocity requires
$$
\frac{n_b}{n_p}\ge\sqrt e\,\frac{v_*\sigma_b^2}{\sigma_p^3}e^{-v_*^2/(2\sigma_p^2)}.
$$
Approximating the background slope by its value at $V$ yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{n_b}{n_p}>C\sqrt{\frac{m_e}{k_{\rm B}T_p}}\frac{VT_b}{T_p}\exp\left(-\frac{m_eV^2}{2k_{\rm B}T_p}\right),\qquad C=\sqrt e.}
$$
Equality is the local marginal-slope condition; strict inequality gives positive resonant drive in this approximation. The numerical $C$ is of order unity and can change when the resonant velocity is optimized or the full wave dispersion is retained.

Replacing $v_*$ by $V$ in the exponential is controlled only if $V\sigma_b/\sigma_p^2\ll1$, in addition to $\sigma_b\ll\sigma_p$. The two width inequalities alone do not guarantee that extra condition far out on a Maxwellian tail. The preceding unapproximated slope inequality remains available when it is not satisfied.

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

Use $k_{\rm B}T_p=1000$ eV and $k_{\rm B}T_b=100$ eV. The nonrelativistic beam speed and cross-sectional area are
$$
V=\sqrt{\frac{2(10\,\mathrm{keV})}{m_e}}\simeq5.93\times10^7\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}},\qquad A_b=\pi(0.5\,\mathrm{mm})^2.
$$
Here $V/\sigma_p=\sqrt{20}$, $\sigma_b^2/\sigma_p^2=0.1$, and the exponential in the requested threshold is $e^{-10}$. With $C=\sqrt e$,
$$
\left(\frac{n_b}{n_p}\right)_{\rm est}=\sqrt e\,(\sqrt{20})(0.1)e^{-10}\simeq3.35\times10^{-5}.
$$
Converting beam density to the magnitude of its <electric current> gives
$$
\boxed{I_{\min,\rm est}=en_bVA_b\simeq2.5\times10^4\,\mathrm A.}
$$
This is an order-of-magnitude estimate from the source's asymptotic formula. The numerical hierarchy is only moderate: $V\sigma_b/\sigma_p^2=\sqrt2$. Retaining the background slope at $V-\sigma_b$ instead gives about $9.1\times10^4$ A as a sufficient positive-slope current at that chosen velocity. The difference quantifies the sensitivity of the exponential-tail approximation; an exact kinetic instability threshold requires the dispersion relation. The requested estimate is therefore on the scale of tens of kiloamperes, rather than a precisely determined current.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Treat the ions as a stationary neutralizing background and displace the cold electrons by a small longitudinal distance $\xi$. Their <electric polarization> is $P=-n_ee\xi$, so the restoring electric field is $E=-P/\epsilon_0=n_ee\xi/\epsilon_0$. The electron equation of motion is
$$
m_e\ddot\xi=-eE=-\frac{n_ee^2}{\epsilon_0}\xi,\qquad
\boxed{\omega_{\rm pe}=\sqrt{\frac{n_ee^2}{m_e\epsilon_0}}.}
$$
For $E=E_0\cos\omega_{\rm pe}t$, the oscillatory velocity is $v=-eE_0\sin\omega_{\rm pe}t/(m_e\omega_{\rm pe})$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\langle u_K\rangle=\frac12n_em_e\langle v^2\rangle=\frac{\epsilon_0E_0^2}{4}=\left\langle\frac{\epsilon_0E^2}{2}\right\rangle.}
$$
The longitudinal mode exchanges electron kinetic energy and electrostatic field energy like a <harmonic oscillator>, whose mean kinetic and potential energies are equal over a cycle. This is a time-average statement for the coherent oscillation, not thermal equipartition with $k_{\rm B}T$.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Debye length> is the distance over which a small, slowly varying electrostatic potential is screened by mobile thermal charges. For classical electrons in a weak potential $\phi$, their <Boltzmann distribution> gives
$$
n_e(\phi)=n_0e^{e\phi/(k_{\rm B}T)}\simeq n_0\left(1+\frac{e\phi}{k_{\rm B}T}\right).
$$
Take fixed singly charged ions of density $n_0$. Then $\rho=e(n_0-n_e)\simeq-n_0e^2\phi/(k_{\rm B}T)$, and <Poisson's equation> outside a test charge becomes $\nabla^2\phi=\phi/\lambda_D^2$, with
$$
\boxed{\lambda_D=\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon_0k_{\rm B}T}{n_ee^2}}.}
$$
The screened point-charge potential is proportional to $e^{-r/\lambda_D}/r$. The derivation assumes nondegenerate statistics and $|e\phi|\ll k_{\rm B}T$. Mobile ions would add their own contribution to $\lambda_D^{-2}$; the expression here is the electron screening length.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

For a nonrelativistic, three-dimensional ideal electron gas with spin degeneracy two,
$$
E_F=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m_e}(3\pi^2n_e)^{2/3},\qquad \omega_{\rm pe}=\sqrt{\frac{n_ee^2}{m_e\epsilon_0}}.
$$
Dividing gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\hbar\omega_{\rm pe}}{E_F}=\sqrt{\frac{4e^2m_e}{\hbar^2\epsilon_0}}(3\pi^2)^{-2/3}n_e^{-1/6}.}
$$
Write the density-independent coefficient as $K$. From $\hbar\omega_{\rm pe}/E_F=1.384$, $n_e=(K/1.384)^6$. In this free-electron model,
$$
\boxed{n_e\simeq1.59\times10^{29}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}},\qquad E_F\simeq10.69\,\mathrm{eV},\qquad \hbar\omega_{\rm pe}\simeq14.79\,\mathrm{eV}.}
$$
The result uses the stipulated energy ratio, rather than substituting a separately tabulated aluminium density or plasmon energy.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Using the replacement prescribed in the question, $k_{\rm B}T\to E_F$, gives
$$
\lambda_D^{\rm prescribed}=\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon_0E_F}{n_ee^2}}\simeq6.10\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm m.
$$
The number of electrons in a <Debye sphere> is consequently
$$
\boxed{N_D=\frac{4\pi}{3}n_e\lambda_D^3\simeq0.151.}
$$
A useful check eliminates $n_e$: with $r=\hbar\omega_{\rm pe}/E_F$ and $k_F=(3\pi^2n_e)^{1/3}$, $k_F\lambda_D=\sqrt2/r$ and $N_D=8\sqrt2/(9\pi r^3)$.

Having substantially fewer than one electron in this sphere shows that the classical many-particle Debye-screening picture is not applicable. It does not exclude collective plasmons in a degenerate electron gas. A proper zero-temperature <Thomas-Fermi screening> calculation uses $\partial n/\partial\mu=3n/(2E_F)$ and gives $\lambda_{\rm TF}^2=2\epsilon_0E_F/(3n_ee^2)$, rather than literally replacing $T$ by $T_F$. The boxed count follows the source's stated prescription and exposes its physical limitation.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume a dilute nondegenerate ideal mixture in thermal and chemical equilibrium, electrical neutrality $N_e=N_p$, and ground-state hydrogen only. Choose the zero of internal energy as a separated proton and electron, so the atom has energy $-R$. Its single-particle partition function contains $e^{R/(k_{\rm B}T)}$:
$$
Z_{{\rm sp},e}=g_eV\left(\frac{2\pi m_ek_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^{3/2},\quad
Z_{{\rm sp},p}=g_pV\left(\frac{2\pi m_pk_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^{3/2},
$$
$$
Z_{{\rm sp},H}=g_HV\left(\frac{2\pi m_Hk_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{R/(k_{\rm B}T)}.
$$
Their ratio has one remaining power of $V$. Neglecting binding mass in $m_H\simeq m_p$ makes the translational mass factor $(m_em_p/m_H)^{3/2}\simeq m_e^{3/2}$. For unresolved ground-state spin states, $g_eg_p/g_H=1$: including proton spin gives $2\times2/4$, or omitting it consistently gives $2\times1/2$. The given law of mass action therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{N_e^2}{N_H}=V\left(\frac{2\pi m_ek_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{-R/(k_{\rm B}T)}.}
$$
This is the <Saha equation> with the degeneracy convention used by the source. Equivalently $n_e^2/n_H=(2\pi m_ek_{\rm B}T/h^2)^{3/2}e^{-R/(k_{\rm B}T)}$.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Define fractional ionization as $x=n_p/(n_H+n_p)$ and use electrical neutrality. For $x=0.01$ and $n_e=10^9\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, $n_H=(1-x)n_e/x=99n_e$. Thus the density form of the <Saha equation> requires
$$
\left(\frac{2\pi m_ek_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{-13.6\,\mathrm{eV}/(k_{\rm B}T)}=\frac{n_e}{99}\simeq1.01\times10^7\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}.
$$
Solving this monotone equation, conveniently after taking its logarithm, gives
$$
\boxed{T\simeq3.48\times10^3\,\mathrm K,\qquad k_{\rm B}T\simeq0.300\,\mathrm{eV}.}
$$
The thermal energy is only about $2.2\%$ of the $13.6$ eV ionization energy. Ionization is not decided by setting $k_{\rm B}T=R$: the high-energy tail and the large translational state-count factor in chemical equilibrium allow a residual ionized fraction at a much lower temperature. This is the equilibrium estimate for the stipulated density and fraction, without inferring an expansion age from it.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Low excited states lie many thermal energies above the ground state at the inferred temperature, so their contributions to the atomic internal partition function are exponentially suppressed. For hydrogen, $E_2-E_1=10.2$ eV and the level degeneracy ratio is $g_2/g_1=4$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{N_{n=2}}{N_{n=1}}=4e^{-10.2\,\mathrm{eV}/(k_{\rm B}T)}\simeq6.8\times10^{-15}.}
$$
The ground-state approximation is therefore excellent for this level under the assumed thermal-equilibrium conditions. A formal sum over infinitely many isolated-atom Rydberg states needs a physical high-level cutoff in a plasma; it should not be used to overturn the finite-level Boltzmann estimate. Radiation-driven nonequilibrium populations would require a different model.

== S18
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Advanced quantum mechanics}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Work with $\eta={\rm diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$ and $\not\!\partial=\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu$. Multiplying the free <Dirac equation> by the operator with the opposite mass sign gives
$$
(i\not\!\partial+m)(i\not\!\partial-m)\psi
=\left[-\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu\partial_\mu\partial_\nu-m^2\right]\psi=0.
$$
The ordinary derivatives commute, so only the symmetric part of $\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu$ contributes. Requiring this operator to factor the <Klein-Gordon equation>, $(\partial^\mu\partial_\mu+m^2)\psi=0$, for arbitrary free momenta gives
$$
\boxed{\{\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu\}=2\eta^{\mu\nu}I,\qquad\{\gamma_\mu,\gamma_\nu\}=2\eta_{\mu\nu}I.}
$$
Thus the <Dirac matrices> represent the corresponding <Clifford algebra>. The inference uses factorization for the general free equation; the fact that one specially chosen wavefunction happens to solve both equations would not alone determine every matrix anticommutator.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $U(x)=e^{ie\alpha(x)}$, $\psi'=U\psi$, and $A'_\mu=A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha$. Direct differentiation in the <gauge covariant derivative> gives
$$
D'_\mu\psi'=(\partial_\mu-ieA'_\mu)(U\psi)
=U\left[\partial_\mu+ie\partial_\mu\alpha-ieA_\mu-ie\partial_\mu\alpha\right]\psi
=UD_\mu\psi.
$$
The gradient of the local phase is exactly cancelled by the potential transformation. Since $U$ is scalar in spinor space and commutes with the <Dirac matrices>,
$$
\boxed{(i\gamma^\mu D'_\mu-m)\psi'=U(i\gamma^\mu D_\mu-m)\psi.}
$$
Consequently a solution is mapped into a solution: the minimally coupled <Dirac equation> is covariant under the local <U(1) gauge symmetry>. The operator and wavefunction both transform; ordinary differentiation of the transformed wavefunction alone would leave an extra phase-gradient term.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Use $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu$. The stated derivative convention gives $[D_\mu,D_\nu]=-ieF_{\mu\nu}$. Also $[\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu]=-2i\sigma^{\mu\nu}$. Splitting products into symmetric and antisymmetric parts therefore yields
$$
(\gamma^\mu D_\mu)^2=D_\mu D^\mu+\frac14[\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu][D_\mu,D_\nu]
=D\cdot D-\frac e2\sigma^{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}.
$$
Multiplying the first-order equation by $i\gamma^\mu D_\mu+m$ now gives
$$
\boxed{\left[-D\cdot D+\frac e2\sigma_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}-m^2\right]\psi=0.}
$$
The antisymmetric derivative commutator is the electromagnetic field strength. It is the extra term absent when squaring an uncoupled Dirac operator with commuting derivatives, and it generates the spin–magnetic-field interaction in the nonrelativistic limit.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

With $A^\mu=(0,\mathbf A)$, the lowered components are $A_\mu=(0,-\mathbf A)$. Define $\boldsymbol\pi=-i\boldsymbol\nabla+e\mathbf A$ and $\mathbf B=\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf A$. This convention describes physical charge $q=-e$. Since $F_{ij}=-\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$,
$$
\sigma_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}=-2\begin{pmatrix}\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf B&0\\0&\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf B\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Writing $\psi=e^{-i(m+E)t}\phi$ makes the squared equation
$$
\left[(m+E)^2-m^2-\boldsymbol\pi^2-e\boldsymbol\Sigma\cdot\mathbf B\right]\phi=0.
$$
Drop $E^2$ relative to $2mE$ and write $\phi=(\varphi,\chi)^T$. Both two-component blocks obey the <Pauli equation>
$$
\boxed{E\varphi=\left[\frac{(\mathbf p+e\mathbf A)^2}{2m}+\frac e{2m}\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf B\right]\varphi,\qquad
E\chi=\left[\frac{(\mathbf p+e\mathbf A)^2}{2m}+\frac e{2m}\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf B\right]\chi.}
$$
The gauge-covariant kinetic term contains $\mathbf p^2/(2m)$, $e(\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf p+\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf A)/(2m)$, and $e^2A^2/(2m)$: free kinetic, orbital magnetic, and diamagnetic contributions. The spin term is $-\boldsymbol\mu\cdot\mathbf B$ with $\boldsymbol\mu=-e\boldsymbol\sigma/(2m)$, giving spin gyromagnetic factor $g=2$ at this order. In uniform $\mathbf B$ and symmetric gauge, the orbital term is $e\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf L/(2m)$.

Squaring introduces extra formal solutions unless the original first-order equation is retained. In the usual positive-energy Dirac basis it relates the small lower component to the upper one by $\chi=(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\boldsymbol\pi)\varphi/(2m+E)\simeq(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\boldsymbol\pi)\varphi/(2m)$. Thus these are not two independent nonrelativistic particles. Restoring units places $\hbar$ in the spin term, $e\hbar\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf B/(2m)$.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Far from a short-range potential, the particle is locally free. The imposed incoming boundary condition is a unit-amplitude plane wave travelling along $+z$, while an outgoing free spherical wave has radial dependence $e^{ikr}/r$. The scattering coefficient can depend on direction, giving
$$
\psi\sim e^{ikz}+f_k(\theta,\phi)\frac{e^{ikr}}r.
$$
For the Schrödinger <probability current>, $\mathbf j=(\hbar/m){\rm Im}(\psi^*\boldsymbol\nabla\psi)$, the incident flux is $j_{\rm in}=\hbar k/m$. The outgoing scattered radial flux, separated from the incident beam, is $j_{\rm sc,r}\sim(\hbar k/m)|f_k|^2/r^2$. Thus the outgoing rate through $r^2d\Omega$, divided by incident flux, gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\frac{j_{\rm sc,r}r^2}{j_{\rm in}}=|f_k(\theta,\phi)|^2.}
$$
This elastic-scattering result uses the same incoming and outgoing wavenumber and the usual outgoing-wave boundary condition.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The spherical potential has a rotationally symmetric <Fourier transform>, so choose the polar axis of the integration along $\mathbf q$. With $q=|\mathbf k_f-\mathbf k_i|=2k\sin(\theta/2)$,
$$
\int_{r'\le r_0}d^3\mathbf r'\,V_0e^{-i\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf r'}
=4\pi V_0\int_0^{r_0}r'^2\frac{\sin(qr')}{qr'}\,dr'
=4\pi V_0\frac{\sin(qr_0)-qr_0\cos(qr_0)}{q^3}.
$$
The <first Born approximation> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{f_k(\theta)=-\frac{2mV_0}{\hbar^2q^3}\left[\sin(qr_0)-qr_0\cos(qr_0)\right],\qquad q=2k\sin\frac\theta2.}
$$
There is no azimuthal dependence. At $q=0$, the bracket is $(qr_0)^3/3+O(q^5)$, so $f_k(0)=-2mV_0r_0^3/(3\hbar^2)$ is finite, not zero. The approximation assumes scattering weak enough for the incident plane wave to replace the distorted wave inside the interaction integral.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Apart from the finite forward limit, amplitude zeros obey $\sin x-x\cos x=0$, or $\tan x=x$, where $x=qr_0$. The first positive root is $x_1\simeq4.49341$. Since the accessible range is $0\le x\le2kr_0$,
$$
\boxed{k_c=\frac{x_1}{2r_0}\simeq\frac{2.247}{r_0}.}
$$
Thus there are no zeros for $k<k_c$; the first enters at the largest momentum transfer, $\theta=\pi$, when $k=k_c$.

For larger roots, $x_n$ lies just below $a_n=\pi(n+\tfrac12)$, $n=1,2,\ldots$. Expanding $\tan(a_n-\varepsilon)\simeq1/\varepsilon$ gives $x_n=a_n-1/a_n+O(a_n^{-3})$. Keeping its leading term yields the requested diffraction estimate
$$
\boxed{\theta_n\simeq2\arcsin\left[\frac\pi{2kr_0}\left(n+\frac12\right)\right],\qquad \pi\left(n+\frac12\right)\le2kr_0.}
$$
Exactly, replace the numerator $\pi(n+\tfrac12)$ by the corresponding root $x_n$. The displayed approximation is a large-$qr_0$ root approximation: taking $k$ large at fixed low order does not make $x_1$ equal to $3\pi/2$. For example, the exact first angle at high $k$ is approximately $4.49341/(kr_0)$, not $4.71239/(kr_0)$.

\Image[2012-s18-born-sphere-form-factor.png]
{title=Born scattering form factor of a spherical constant potential}
{description=The exact normalized amplitude and its first three zeros are shown. The asymptotic half-integer-pi locations are close estimates, with a visible low-order correction.}

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume the scalar potential is real, so the <Weyl equation> has a Hermitian Hamiltonian $H=\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf p+V$. Multiplying the equation and its adjoint by $\psi^\dagger$ and $\psi$ respectively gives
$$
\partial_t(\psi^\dagger\psi)=-\boldsymbol\nabla\cdot(\psi^\dagger\boldsymbol\sigma\psi).
$$
Thus with $\rho=\psi^\dagger\psi$ and $\mathbf J=\psi^\dagger\boldsymbol\sigma\psi$, the lower-index four-current $j_\mu=(\rho,-\mathbf J)$ obeys $\boxed{\partial^\mu j_\mu=0}$.

For $K=\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf p$,
$$
[K,H]=[K,V]=-i\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\boldsymbol\nabla V.
$$
Consequently $K$ is conserved for spatially constant $V$; a spatially uniform time-dependent scalar offset would also commute with it. For general nonuniform $V$ it is not conserved. At nonzero momentum, $(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\hat{\mathbf p})^2=I$, so the <helicity> eigenvalues are $\boxed{+1,-1}$. In the chosen constant-potential Weyl Hamiltonian, the positive $E-V$ branch has helicity $+1$; the negative branch has $-1$. An expectation value in a superposition need not itself be an eigenvalue.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For dependence on $z$ alone and constant $V_0$, remove the common phase by $\psi=e^{-iV_0t}\widetilde\psi$. Then $\partial_t\widetilde\psi=-\sigma_z\partial_z\widetilde\psi$. The normalized eigenvectors
$$
\boxed{u_+=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix},\qquad u_-=\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}}
$$
obey $\sigma_zu_\pm=\pm u_\pm$. The corresponding first-order transport equations have characteristics $t-z$ and $t+z$, respectively, giving
$$
\boxed{\psi(z,t)=e^{-iV_0t}\left[\eta(t-z)u_++\rho(t+z)u_-\right].}
$$
Here $\eta$ propagates toward $+z$ and $\rho$ toward $-z$, both at unit speed.

For a fixed unit vector $\hat{\mathbf n}$, replace $z$ by $\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot\mathbf x$ and choose orthonormal spinors satisfying $(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\hat{\mathbf n})u_\pm(\hat{\mathbf n})=\pm u_\pm(\hat{\mathbf n})$. If $\hat{\mathbf n}=(\sin\vartheta\cos\varphi,\sin\vartheta\sin\varphi,\cos\vartheta)$, one choice is
$$
u_+(\hat{\mathbf n})=\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\vartheta/2)\\ e^{i\varphi}\sin(\vartheta/2)\end{pmatrix},\qquad
u_-(\hat{\mathbf n})=\begin{pmatrix}-e^{-i\varphi}\sin(\vartheta/2)\\\cos(\vartheta/2)\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Independent constant phases on the spinors can be absorbed into the two arbitrary envelope functions.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Evaluate the envelopes at their displayed arguments and set $a=\eta^*\rho$. The common phase $e^{-iV_0t}$ cancels from every bilinear. Direct use of the <Pauli matrices> gives
$$
\boxed{j_\mu=\left(|\eta|^2+|\rho|^2,\;-2\operatorname{Re}a,\;-2\operatorname{Im}a,\;|\rho|^2-|\eta|^2\right).}
$$
The physical longitudinal current is $J_z=|\eta|^2-|\rho|^2$: the two density profiles move in opposite directions. Their separate transport identities give $\partial_t(|\eta|^2+|\rho|^2)+\partial_z(|\eta|^2-|\rho|^2)=0$.

For a coherent superposition, the transverse currents $J_x=2\operatorname{Re}(\eta^*\rho)$ and $J_y=2\operatorname{Im}(\eta^*\rho)$ generally do not vanish. They are independent of $x,y$, so they contribute no divergence in this one-dimensional construction. Omitting them would give an incorrect local four-current. Pointwise, $|\mathbf J|^2=(|\eta|^2+|\rho|^2)^2$, so the pure two-component Weyl current is future null wherever the density is nonzero. If only one envelope is present, the current points entirely along its propagation direction; an incoherent average can remove the transverse interference terms.

== S19
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Particle accelerator science}

=== 1
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

Use transverse coordinates $(x,x')$ and a signed focusing strength $K$ in <Hill's equation>, $x''+Kx=0$. A drift of length $\ell$ has <beam transfer matrix>
$$
D(\ell)=\begin{pmatrix}1&\ell\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For a uniform focusing <quadrupole magnet> with $K=\kappa^2>0$, and a defocusing one with $K=-\kappa^2$,
$$
\boxed{Q_F(\ell)=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\kappa\ell&\sin(\kappa\ell)/\kappa\\-\kappa\sin\kappa\ell&\cos\kappa\ell\end{pmatrix},\quad
Q_D(\ell)=\begin{pmatrix}\cosh\kappa\ell&\sinh(\kappa\ell)/\kappa\\\kappa\sinh\kappa\ell&\cosh\kappa\ell\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The focusing plane has oscillatory trajectories, while the defocusing plane has hyperbolic growth and decay. A magnetic quadrupole focuses one transverse plane and defocuses the other; reversing its gradient interchanges their roles.

If $|K|\ell^2\ll1$ and the element's internal drift can be neglected or assigned separately, its kick may be concentrated at one position. With $1/f=|K|\ell$,
$$
\boxed{F=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-1/f&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad G=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1/f&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Both kicks preserve position and change the slope linearly with displacement. All these ideal uncoupled maps have determinant one.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Maps act on column vectors, so the first element appears at the right of the product. Starting immediately before the focusing kick, one full cell has
$$
M_x=D(L_2)G D(L_1)F.
$$
Multiplying gives
$$
\boxed{M_x=\begin{pmatrix}
1-L_1/f-L_1L_2/f^2&L_1+L_2+L_1L_2/f\\
-L_1/f^2&1+L_1/f
\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
It has $\det M_x=1$, as required for this lossless linear <beam optics>. The other transverse plane experiences the opposite quadrupole signs, so
$$
\boxed{M_y=M_x\big|_{f\to-f}=\begin{pmatrix}
1+L_1/f-L_1L_2/f^2&L_1+L_2-L_1L_2/f\\
-L_1/f^2&1-L_1/f
\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The two planes have different envelopes but the same trace and therefore the same stable phase-advance magnitude for this equal-strength doublet.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

For a real determinant-one map, bounded generic repeated transport requires eigenvalues $e^{\pm i\mu}$, equivalently $|\operatorname{tr}M|<2$. Here both planes have $\operatorname{tr}M=2-L_1L_2/f^2$, so
$$
\boxed{0<\frac{L_1L_2}{f^2}<4,\qquad \cos\mu=1-\frac{L_1L_2}{2f^2}.}
$$
For positive drift lengths and $f>0$, this means $f>\sqrt{L_1L_2}/2$. The equalities are marginal and generally produce a Jordan-block growth rather than stable motion. Moving the reference point changes the map by a similarity transformation, $M\to PMP^{-1}$, so the eigenvalues and full-cell phase advance are unchanged.

Write the stable map using the <Courant-Snyder parameters>, also called Twiss parameters:
$$
M=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\mu+\alpha\sin\mu&\beta\sin\mu\\-\gamma_T\sin\mu&\cos\mu-\alpha\sin\mu\end{pmatrix},\qquad \beta\gamma_T-\alpha^2=1.
$$
At the chosen reference point $\beta=M_{12}/\sin\mu$, $\alpha=(M_{11}-M_{22})/(2\sin\mu)$, and $\gamma_T=-M_{21}/\sin\mu$. Repeated traversal gives
$$
\boxed{M^n=\begin{pmatrix}\cos n\mu+\alpha\sin n\mu&\beta\sin n\mu\\-\gamma_T\sin n\mu&\cos n\mu-\alpha\sin n\mu\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
If the full ring contains $N_c$ identical cells, its one-turn phase is $N_c\mu$ modulo $2\pi$; $n$ ring turns use $nN_c\mu$. The displayed $M^n$ is for $n$ repetitions of the specified cell.

\Image[2012-s19-cell-stability.png]
{title=Stability and phase advance of the equal-strength quadrupole cell}
{description=Both transverse planes have trace 2 minus L_1 L_2/f squared. The strict stable interval excludes the two marginal boundaries.}

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Relativistic radiation is concentrated into an angle of order $1/\gamma$ around the instantaneous <velocity>. The emission direction sweeps past a tangent observer over an orbital interval of order $\rho/(\gamma c)$. Since the charge moves toward the observer, arrival times are compressed by an additional factor of order $1/\gamma^2$, giving the spectral scale $\omega\sim c\gamma^3/\rho$.

The conventional numerical factor follows from the radiation Fourier phase. Near the tangent direction, circular motion gives
$$
\omega\left(t-\frac{\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot\mathbf r(t)}c\right)\simeq\omega\left(\frac{t}{2\gamma^2}+\frac{c^2t^3}{6\rho^2}\right).
$$
With $u=c\gamma t/\rho$, this becomes $[\omega\rho/(2c\gamma^3)](u+u^3/3)$. The resulting Airy-type integrals are expressible through modified Bessel functions with tangent-plane argument $\xi=\omega\rho/(3c\gamma^3)$. The standard angle-integrated <synchrotron radiation> spectrum has shape
$$
F(x)=x\int_x^\infty K_{5/3}(z)\,dz,\qquad x=\frac\omega{\omega_c},\qquad\xi=\frac{x}{2}.
$$
It therefore identifies
$$
\boxed{\omega_c=\frac{3c\gamma^3}{2\rho},\qquad\epsilon_c=\hbar\omega_c=\frac32\frac{\hbar c}{\rho}\gamma^3.}
$$
The beaming argument supplies the physical scaling; the spectral integral fixes the conventional factor $3/2$. Critical energy is a broad spectral scale, not a hard photon-energy cutoff.<Synchrotron Radiation in the Geant4 Physics Reference Manual by Geant4 Collaboration>[Critical energy and critical mode number]{ref}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

For a $5$ GeV electron, $\gamma_e=E/(m_ec^2)\simeq9785$. With $\rho=10$ m, the stated <synchrotron critical frequency> formula gives
$$
\boxed{\epsilon_{c,e}\simeq2.77\times10^4\,\mathrm{eV}=27.7\,\mathrm{keV}.}
$$
At the same total beam energy and bend radius, a proton has $\gamma_p=5/0.9383\simeq5.329$, and the same supplied formula gives
$$
\boxed{\epsilon_{c,p}\simeq4.48\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{eV}.}
$$
The ratio is $(\gamma_p/\gamma_e)^3\simeq(m_e/m_p)^3$, explaining the strong suppression for protons. The critical-energy formula is an ultrarelativistic approximation, excellent for the electron and only approximate for this moderately relativistic proton. If “5 GeV proton beam” instead denotes kinetic energy, then $\gamma_p=1+5/0.9383$ and the estimate is about $7.50\times10^{-6}$ eV; the two beam-energy conventions should not be mixed.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Substituting the electron Lorentz factor in the supplied energy-loss formula gives
$$
\boxed{U_0=\frac{e^2\gamma_e^4}{3\epsilon_0\rho}\simeq5.53\,\mathrm{MeV\ per\ turn}.}
$$
Without replenishment, the electron's momentum decreases. The reference dipoles then bend it more tightly, producing a displaced orbit. In linear <beam optics>, $x=D_x\delta$ and $y=D_y\delta$, where $\delta=\Delta p/p\simeq-U_0/E\simeq-1.11\times10^{-3}$ per uncompensated turn. In an ideal planar ring the horizontal dispersion is nonzero and the orbit shifts horizontally, inward where $D_x>0$. There is no corresponding vertical closed-orbit shift when $D_y=0$. Real coupling or vertical dispersion can change that idealization.

With current $I=0.500$ A, the number of circulating particles is $N=IT_{\rm rev}/e$, so the radiated power is
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{NU_0}{T_{\rm rev}}=\frac{IU_0}{e}\simeq2.76\,\mathrm{MW}.}
$$
The $2\,\mu\mathrm s$ revolution time cancels when using beam current. Radio-frequency cavities restore the lost energy longitudinally, with synchronous gain $eV_{\rm RF}\sin\varphi_s=U_0$ in the sine-phase convention. Their RF power system must supply at least this beam power, in addition to its own losses. Radiation damping and quantum excitation determine beam emittances; they should not be confused with the dispersive closed-orbit shift from a momentum change.

=== 3
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== i
{scope}

====== Solution

The quoted proton energies are explicitly kinetic. For mean ring radius $\bar R=215$ m,
$$
C=2\pi\bar R,\qquad\gamma=1+\frac{K}{0.9383\,\mathrm{GeV}},\qquad\beta=\sqrt{1-\gamma^{-2}},\qquad f_{\rm rev}=\frac{\beta c}{C}.
$$
Using these expressions rather than setting $\beta=1$ at injection gives
$$
\boxed{f_{\rm rev}(4\,\mathrm{GeV})\simeq217.880\,\mathrm{kHz},\quad f_{\rm rev}(20\,\mathrm{GeV})\simeq221.700\,\mathrm{kHz},\quad f_{\rm rev}(50\,\mathrm{GeV})\simeq221.885\,\mathrm{kHz}.}
$$
The frequency approaches $c/C$ as energy increases; it is the velocity increase, not a change in the prescribed mean ring circumference, that produces the frequency sweep.

===== ii
{scope}

====== Solution

The <RF harmonic number> is the number of RF cycles per revolution, so $f_{\rm RF}=h f_{\rm rev}$. For $h=32$,
$$
\boxed{f_{\rm RF}(4\,\mathrm{GeV})\simeq6.97217\,\mathrm{MHz},\qquad f_{\rm RF}(50\,\mathrm{GeV})\simeq7.10033\,\mathrm{MHz}.}
$$
The synchronous phase fixes which phase of that voltage is sampled, not the frequency ratio. For a sine voltage, $\varphi_s=0$ gives zero net accelerating voltage and describes a stationary ideal-energy condition; an actual energy ramp requires a phase producing the required energy gain. A different phase-origin convention changes that statement about the zero of phase, but not the boxed frequencies.

==== b
{scope}

===== i
{scope}

====== Solution

The instantaneous <beam lifetime> is
$$
\boxed{\tau=-\frac{N}{dN/dt},}
$$
where losses make $dN/dt<0$. If the fractional loss rate is constant, $N(t)=N_0e^{-t/\tau}$ and $\tau$ is the time to fall to $1/e$ of the starting population. At fixed revolution frequency, the circulating current has the same exponential decay. Density-dependent loss mechanisms can make the instantaneous lifetime vary, so a single exponential need not fit every fill.

===== ii
{scope}

====== Solution

Particles are lost when a process drives their transverse amplitude or energy outside the machine's accepted region. Principal causes include scattering from residual gas; <Touschek scattering> within a bunch, which can convert transverse momentum into excessive longitudinal momentum; collision losses at an interaction point; diffusion from nonlinear resonances, field or RF noise; and collective instabilities or insufficient RF acceptance. Mechanical aperture restrictions turn large-amplitude motion into a loss.

Synchrotron-radiation energy loss is normally replenished by RF, so it does not by itself remove one electron for each emitted photon. Rare large energy deviations can nevertheless contribute to losses if they exceed the accepted energy range. Continuous top-up injection can hide the underlying decay and must be accounted for when measuring a lifetime.

===== iii
{scope}

====== Solution

Use a calibrated, nonintercepting <DC beam current transformer> to measure the circulating current repeatedly during a fill, with top-up injection absent or separately recorded. At fixed revolution frequency, $I=eNf_{\rm rev}$, so a fit to $\log I$ versus time gives slope $-1/\tau$ for exponential decay. The transformer's zero offset and long-term drift must be controlled, especially at low current. An AC current transformer or a calibrated bunch pickup can additionally resolve bunch charge, whereas a DC transformer provides the total circulating intensity needed here.

==== c
{scope}

===== i
{scope}

====== Solution

Both machines are proposed electron–positron <linear colliders>, using sources, damping rings, bunch compressors, two opposing main linacs, and final-focus and beam-delivery sections that bring very small bunches to an interaction point. The spent beams go to extraction lines and beam dumps rather than making repeated collision turns.

In the exam-era <International Linear Collider> reference design, polarized electrons and an undulator-based positron source feed damping rings and two superconducting main linacs; the full $500$ GeV centre-of-mass facility is about $31$ km long. The <Compact Linear Collider> adds high-current drive-beam generation and deceleration lines parallel to its main linacs; its $3$ TeV concept is roughly $48$ km long. The linear layout avoids the repeated synchrotron-radiation losses that would be severe for multi-hundred-GeV electrons in a storage ring. https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.2361[ILC Reference Design Report, Volume 3—Accelerator (2007), abstract and overall layout]{ref} https://cds.cern.ch/record/1500095[CLIC Conceptual Design Report, CERN-2012-007, accelerator concept and layout]{ref}

===== ii
{scope}

====== Solution

For a comparison appropriate to this 2012 examination, use the reference-design and conceptual-design targets rather than later revised starting stages:
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Design}&\sqrt{s}&\mathcal L\;(\mathrm{cm^{-2}s^{-1}})\\\hline
\text{ILC reference design}&500\,\mathrm{GeV}&2\times10^{34}\\
\text{CLIC multi-TeV design}&3\,\mathrm{TeV}&5.9\times10^{34}
\end{array}}
$$
The ILC reference programme included scanning approximately $200$–$500$ GeV and a possible $1$ TeV upgrade. The CLIC 2012 studies included lower-energy construction stages as well as the ultimate multi-TeV machine. These are design goals, not achieved operating luminosities.

<Collider luminosity> sets the event rate for a process of cross-section $\sigma$ through $dN/dt=\mathcal L\sigma$; it is not radiated optical power. https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.2361[ILC Reference Design Report, Volume 3 (2007), design energy and peak luminosity in the abstract]{ref} https://indico.cern.ch/event/216895/contributions/442123/attachments/347325/484269/lucaci_FCAL.pdf[CLIC CDR status, November 2012, slide 33: staged energies and total luminosities]{ref}

===== iii
{scope}

====== Solution

The <International Linear Collider> uses externally powered superconducting niobium RF cavities at about $1.3$ GHz, with a reference average accelerating gradient of $31.5$ MV/m. Superconducting walls reduce RF dissipation, allowing relatively long RF pulses and bunch trains, at the cost of cryogenic infrastructure.

The <Compact Linear Collider> uses normal-conducting copper structures at about $12$ GHz and a main-linac gradient near $100$ MV/m in its multi-TeV design. A separate high-current, lower-energy electron drive beam is decelerated in power-extraction structures; the resulting RF power accelerates the high-energy, low-current main beams. This two-beam concept supplies short, high-power pulses without requiring a high-power klystron at every main-beam structure.

**ILC: superconducting low-loss RF; CLIC: high-gradient normal-conducting two-beam RF.** Both rely on damping and final focusing to obtain very small collision spots, so energy alone does not determine their luminosity. https://cds.cern.ch/record/1072192/files/ab-2007-078.pdf[ILC reference gradient and superconducting RF baseline]{ref} https://indico.cern.ch/event/216895/contributions/442123/attachments/347325/484269/lucaci_FCAL.pdf[CLIC CDR status, November 2012, slide 4: copper cavities, 12 GHz, 100 MV/m and drive-beam power generation]{ref}

== S25
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Physics of climate change}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Spectral irradiance> $F_\lambda$ is radiant power crossing unit area per unit wavelength, integrated over the relevant hemisphere with the projected-area factor: $F_\lambda=\int I_\lambda\cos\theta\,d\Omega$. Its units are $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ per unit wavelength; <spectral radiance> $I_\lambda$ additionally refers to unit solid angle. For an isotropic outgoing hemisphere with <Planck function> $B_\lambda$, the integral is $F_\lambda=\pi B_\lambda$.

The vertical <optical depth> above height $z$ is the dimensionless extinction integral
$$
\tau_\lambda(z)=\int_z^\infty n_a(z')\sigma_\lambda(z')\,dz',\qquad
\boxed{\frac{d\tau_\lambda}{dz}=-n_a\sigma_\lambda=-\kappa_\lambda\rho_a.}
$$
Here $z$ increases upward, $n_a$ is absorber <number density>, $\sigma_\lambda$ its absorption cross section, $\rho_a$ its <mass density>, and $\kappa_\lambda$ the absorption coefficient per unit absorber mass. Equivalently, using bulk air <density> $\rho$ and absorber mass fraction $q_a$, $d\tau_\lambda/dz=-\kappa_\lambda q_a\rho$. The coefficient may depend on <temperature>, <pressure> and wavelength. For purely absorbing gas, transmission along a ray is $e^{-\tau_{\rm ray}}$; scattering, if present, contributes to extinction and requires its own source term.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the optical-depth weighting stipulated in the paper. A jump in the <Planck function> at $\tau_1$ gives $dB/d\tau=(B_1-B_0)\delta(\tau-\tau_1)$, so
$$
\boxed{I_0=\pi\left[B_0+(B_1-B_0)e^{-\tau_1}\right]
=\pi B_0(1-e^{-\tau_1})+\pi B_1e^{-\tau_1}.}
$$
The upper isothermal layer emits $\pi B_0(1-e^{-\tau_1})$ and transmits the lower region's blackbody <spectral irradiance> with factor $e^{-\tau_1}$. The lower layer and black surface have the same $B_1$, so their combined radiation entering the upper layer is $\pi B_1$, independent of how its emission is divided between the lower gas and surface.

More generally, integration by parts rewrites the supplied expression as
$$
I_0=\pi B(\tau_s)e^{-\tau_s}+\pi\int_0^{\tau_s}B(\tau)e^{-\tau}\,d\tau.
$$
The terms are attenuated surface emission and the atmosphere's own emission. In the original boundary-plus-gradient form, $\pi B_0$ is the isothermal reference flux and the integral corrects for temperature variation below the top. Optical depths of order unity contribute most strongly. These formulas follow the paper's specified exponential weighting; a full angular <radiative transfer> calculation uses ray-angle-dependent optical paths rather than identifying every ray with a vertical path.

==== 3
{scope}

===== a
{scope}

====== Solution

For the linear <Planck function> profile, direct integration gives
$$
I_0=\pi B_0+\pi\alpha(1-e^{-\tau_s})\longrightarrow\boxed{\pi(B_0+\alpha)}
$$
in the optically thick limit. A sudden doubling of absorber abundance doubles <optical depth> at every physical height while initially leaving the <temperature> profile fixed. With $\widetilde\tau=2\tau$, the same profile is $B=B_0+(\alpha/2)\widetilde\tau$, not $B_0+\alpha\widetilde\tau$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\widetilde I_0=\pi(B_0+\alpha/2),\qquad \Delta I_0=-\frac{\pi\alpha}{2}.}
$$
If temperature increases downward, $\alpha>0$ and outgoing <spectral irradiance> decreases immediately because the effective emitting region moves upward into cooler gas. Subsequent thermal adjustment is outside this instantaneous comparison.

===== b
{scope}

====== Solution

For the quadratic <Planck function> profile,
$$
I_0=\pi B_0+2\pi\beta\int_0^{\tau_s}\tau e^{-\tau}\,d\tau
=\pi B_0+2\pi\beta[1-(1+\tau_s)e^{-\tau_s}].
$$
The optically thick limit and the instantaneous doubled-abundance result are therefore
$$
\boxed{I_0=\pi(B_0+2\beta),\qquad
\widetilde I_0=\pi(B_0+\beta/2),\qquad
\Delta I_0=-\frac32\pi\beta.}
$$
The new coefficient is $\beta/4$, since $B(\widetilde\tau)=B_0+\beta(\widetilde\tau/2)^2$ at fixed physical <temperature>. For positive $\beta$, the outgoing <spectral irradiance> decreases. The factor differs from the linear case because the temperature-dependent source function curves with depth; doubling abundance does not mean holding its old functional coefficients fixed in the new optical-depth coordinate.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Hydrostatic equilibrium> balances the vertical <pressure gradient> against weight, $dP/dz=-\rho g$. For a dry <ideal gas> parcel displaced slowly enough to maintain pressure balance but rapidly enough to exchange negligible <heat>, the first law gives
$$
c_p\,dT-\frac1\rho\,dP=0.
$$
Combining the two equations yields the <dry adiabatic lapse rate>
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_d=-\frac{dT}{dz}=\frac g{c_p}\simeq9.8\,\mathrm{K\,km^{-1}},}
$$
using $c_p\simeq1004\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Here <temperature> in the thermodynamic differential is absolute, $g$ and $c_p$ are treated as constant, composition is fixed, and condensation is excluded. Hydrostatic balance alone does not force the atmosphere to be adiabatic; the parcel assumption supplies the extra relation.

Water condensation releases <latent heat>, reducing the cooling of a rising saturated parcel and giving a smaller <moist adiabatic lapse rate>. Radiation, mixing, large-scale motion and incomplete convective adjustment also shape the environmental lapse rate. Thus an observed $6$--$7\,\mathrm{K\,km^{-1}}$ need not equal the dry parcel value and is broadly consistent with a moist, radiatively influenced atmosphere.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Write $s=T_s-273.15\,\mathrm K$ as the numerical sea-level Celsius <temperature> in degrees Celsius, and use the same degree increments for $\Gamma z$. The model's top temperature is $T_C=s-\Gamma z$. For positive $\alpha$ its intended cold-ice branch has $T_C<0$, so the positive melt-loss magnitude and snow gain are
$$
L(z)=\frac{\alpha}{\Gamma z-s},\qquad A(z)=1-\beta z,\qquad
F(z)=\dot M=A(z)-L(z).
$$
The model is singular at $z=s/\Gamma$ and is not a physical melting law across that boundary. On the relevant interval $s/\Gamma<z<1/\beta$, $L$ decreases hyperbolically and $A$ decreases linearly. Assume a fixed area and positive ice density, so increasing <mass> increases $z$: stability has the same sign as $F'(z)$.

The equilibrium equation is
$$
(1-\beta z)(\Gamma z-s)=\alpha,
$$
with roots
$$
\boxed{z_\pm=\frac{\Gamma+\beta s\pm\sqrt{(\Gamma-\beta s)^2-4\alpha\beta\Gamma}}{2\beta\Gamma}.}
$$
Two distinct physical equilibria, one stable, exist provided
$$
\boxed{\Gamma-\beta s>2\sqrt{\alpha\beta\Gamma}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
s<\frac\Gamma\beta-2\sqrt{\frac{\alpha\Gamma}{\beta}}.}
$$
This combines a positive allowed interval with a strictly positive discriminant. At the larger root $F'<0$ and small perturbations restore the ice cap; the smaller root has $F'>0$ and is unstable. Since
$$
F'(z)=-\beta+\frac{\alpha\Gamma}{(\Gamma z-s)^2},
$$
a restoring local response requires
$$
\boxed{z>z_{\rm slope}=\frac s\Gamma+\sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{\beta\Gamma}}.}
$$
This is the lower height bound from the stability derivative; the stable equilibrium itself is $z_+$. The minimum initial height for persistence, a different notion of threshold, is the unstable separator $z_-$: initial heights above it approach $z_+$, while lower heights lose ice and leave the model's cold branch. At equality in the discriminant condition the two equilibria merge, giving a marginal fold rather than a strictly stable ice cap.

\Image[2012-s25-ice-cap-equilibria.png]
{title=Snow accumulation, melt loss and the ice-cap stability threshold}
{description=The initial curves have an unstable lower intersection and a stable upper intersection. Slow sea-level warming shifts the melt curve until both equilibria meet at a fold; all temperatures inside the reciprocal melt law are Celsius.}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

At $z=4\,\mathrm{km}$, the snow gain is $1-0.2(4)=0.2$ and the model's top Celsius <temperature> is $275-273.15-6(4)=-22.15^\circ\mathrm C$. Equilibrium gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha=0.2(22.15)=4.43^\circ\mathrm C}
$$
in the normalized rate convention. Using $273\,\mathrm K$ for the Celsius zero instead would give $4.4^\circ\mathrm C$. The local slope is $-0.2+4.43(6)/(22.15)^2\simeq-0.1458\,\mathrm{km^{-1}}<0$, as required. Initially $z_-=1.3083\,\mathrm{km}$ and $z_{\rm slope}\simeq2.230\,\mathrm{km}$, while $z_+=4\,\mathrm{km}$.

As $T_s$ slowly rises, melt loss increases at fixed $z$, the stable high branch moves downward and the unstable branch upward. They merge at
$$
s_{\rm crit}=\frac6{0.2}-2\sqrt{\frac{4.43(6)}{0.2}}\simeq6.944^\circ\mathrm C,
$$
$$
\boxed{T_{s,\rm crit}\simeq280.09\,\mathrm K,\qquad
\Delta T_s\simeq5.09\,\mathrm K,\qquad z_{\rm fold}\simeq3.079\,\mathrm{km}.}
$$
Beyond the fold the model has no steady cold ice cap: its net balance is negative throughout the physical interval and ice shrinks until the assumed law ceases to apply. Quasistatic tracking presumes adjustment is fast compared with the imposed warming, an assumption that deteriorates near the fold as the restoring slope tends to zero. This singular idealized law is not a quantitative real-world melting forecast.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Complete oxidation of $10^{12}\,\mathrm{kg}$ of carbon adds $n_C=10^{12}/(12\times10^{-3})=8.33\times10^{13}\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <carbon dioxide>. With atmospheric <mass> about $5.1\times10^{18}\,\mathrm{kg}$ and mean air molar mass $28.97\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{kg\,mol^{-1}}$, there are about $1.76\times10^{20}\,\mathrm{mol}$ of air. The dry-air mole fraction, approximately the volume fraction, changes by
$$
\boxed{\Delta C\simeq10^6\frac{8.33\times10^{13}}{1.76\times10^{20}}\simeq0.47\,\mathrm{ppmv\ per\ GtC}.}
$$
It is the carbon inventory that counts: oxidation adds oxygen to each molecule but does not change the number of carbon-containing molecules. Using $5.15\times10^{18}\,\mathrm{kg}$ instead changes the estimate by only about one percent. The atmospheric constants are standard Earth estimates, not additional data read from a missing plot.https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/earthfact.html[terrestrial atmosphere: total mass and mean molecular weight]{ref}

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The term $b_1E$ is the effective atmospheric addition from ongoing emissions. Its coefficient $0.25\,\mathrm{ppmv/GtC}$ is about half the full-retention estimate $0.47\,\mathrm{ppmv/GtC}$, so the model implicitly removes or partitions some emitted carbon promptly. The term $-b_0(C-C_0)$ represents slower net uptake of the excess by other reservoirs; its <relaxation time> is $b_0^{-1}=200$ years. Both terms have units of ppmv per year.

Thousands of years at constant $E_0$ erase the initial transient, giving
$$
0=b_1E_0-b_0(C-C_0),\qquad
\boxed{C(1800)=C_0+\frac{b_1E_0}{b_0}=279+1=280\,\mathrm{ppmv}.}
$$
The reference $C_0$ is the zero-anthropogenic-emission equilibrium, not the equilibrium when the stated small persistent emissions are present.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Put $A=b_1E_0/b_0$. The proposed solution can be written
$$
C-C_0=\frac A{g+b_0}\left(ge^{-b_0t}+b_0e^{gt}\right).
$$
It gives $C(0)-C_0=A$. Differentiation and substitution give, respectively,
$$
\dot C=\frac{Agb_0}{g+b_0}\left(e^{gt}-e^{-b_0t}\right),
$$
$$
b_1E_0e^{gt}-b_0(C-C_0)=\frac{Agb_0}{g+b_0}\left(e^{gt}-e^{-b_0t}\right),
$$
so it satisfies both the differential equation and the initial condition. At $t=200$ years,
$$
\boxed{C(2000)=279+\frac{0.03e^{-1}+0.005e^6}{0.035}\simeq336.95\,\mathrm{ppmv},}
$$
with $E(2000)=0.02e^6\simeq8.069\,\mathrm{GtC\,yr^{-1}}$. The earlier emissions grow on the $g^{-1}=33.3$ year timescale, much faster than the model's 200-year removal time.

==== 4
{scope}

===== a
{scope}

====== Solution

Let $t=0$ now denote the year 2000 and $C_{00}=336.95\,\mathrm{ppmv}$. When emissions stop, the model gives
$$
\boxed{C(t)=C_0+(C_{00}-C_0)e^{-t/(200\,\mathrm{yr})}.}
$$
The concentration peaks at the switch, then returns exponentially to $279\,\mathrm{ppmv}$; in 2500 it is about $283.76\,\mathrm{ppmv}$. Before 2000 both scenarios share the exponential-emissions trajectory.

Using the specified logarithmic equilibrium warming relative to $C_0$,
$$
\Delta T_{\rm eq}(C)=3\log_2(C/C_0)\,\mathrm K,
$$
gives the fast-adjustment peak estimate
$$
\boxed{\Delta T_{\rm peak}\approx3\log_2(336.95/279)\simeq0.82\,\mathrm K.}
$$
An unspecified finite thermal response time below twenty years delays and reduces that peak relative to the instantaneous-equilibrium value; its exact amount cannot be fixed without a response law and a chosen thermal time. The carbon-removal time is much longer, so the warming subsequently declines gradually in this model. Taking the 1800 concentration $280$ as the temperature reference instead changes the quoted estimate by only $0.016\,\mathrm K$.

\Image[2012-s25-carbon-emission-scenarios.png]
{title=The two specified carbon-emission scenarios from 1800 to 2500}
{description=Both curves share the prescribed emissions growth up to 2000. Zero later emissions give exponential return to 279 ppmv; holding emissions constant gives approach to 682.43 ppmv. The inset-free lower panel shows equilibrium warming rather than an invented thermal adjustment history.}

===== b
{scope}

====== Solution

Holding emissions at $E_{00}=8.069\,\mathrm{GtC\,yr^{-1}}$ gives equilibrium concentration
$$
C_\infty=C_0+\frac{b_1E_{00}}{b_0}\simeq682.43\,\mathrm{ppmv}.
$$
For time $t$ since 2000,
$$
\boxed{C(t)=C_\infty+(C_{00}-C_\infty)e^{-t/(200\,\mathrm{yr})}.}
$$
It increases monotonically on the same 200-year timescale and reaches about $654.07\,\mathrm{ppmv}$ in 2500. The limiting, and hence peak asymptotic, warming is
$$
\boxed{\Delta T_{\rm peak}\longrightarrow3\log_2(682.43/279)\simeq3.87\,\mathrm K.}
$$
In 2500 the equilibrium warming is about $3.69\,\mathrm K$, still below that asymptote. A stable thermal adjustment much faster than the carbon timescale follows this slow approach with a short lag and has the same limiting warming. These are predictions of the specified linear reservoir model, not a claim that constant real emissions produce a finite universal atmospheric equilibrium.

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

The real <carbon cycle> has multiple reservoirs and adjustment times, rather than one universally exponential removal process. Air-sea exchange, ocean mixing and carbonate chemistry give a long-lived atmospheric tail; uptake also changes with <temperature>, circulation and carbon loading. For example, a warming ocean can absorb less additional <carbon dioxide>, so a fixed $b_0$ omits a potentially positive climate-carbon feedback. Terrestrial vegetation and soil responses provide further examples. **One missing feature is the dependence of carbon uptake on the changing state of the ocean and climate.**

== S26
{c}
{scope}
{title2=Stars and galaxies}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

For a spherical star in <hydrostatic equilibrium>, $dP/dr=-Gm(r)\rho/r^2$. Multiply by $4\pi r^3dr$ and integrate to its radius $R$:
$$
\int_0^R4\pi r^3\frac{dP}{dr}\,dr=-\int_0^R\frac{Gm(r)}r\,dm=\Omega.
$$
Integration by parts gives $4\pi R^3P_s-3\int P\,dV=\Omega$. Neglecting the external surface pressure $P_s$, an <ideal gas> with constant <heat capacity ratio> $\gamma$ has thermal <energy density> $u=P/(\gamma-1)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{3(\gamma-1)U+\Omega=0,\qquad U=\int u\,dV.}
$$
This is the hydrostatic <virial theorem>. A nonzero confining pressure retains the right-hand side $4\pi R^3P_s$; varying composition, radiation contributions or a spatially varying effective exponent require the integral relation, rather than pulling one $\gamma$ outside it.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For a fully ionized, nonrelativistic monatomic <ideal gas>, $\gamma=5/3$ and the <virial theorem> becomes
$$
\boxed{\Omega=-2U,\qquad E=U+\Omega=-U.}
$$
Since $U$ increases with its particle-weighted mean <temperature>, the equilibrated self-gravitating object has <negative heat capacity>: adding total <energy> allows expansion, makes $\Omega$ less negative, and reduces $U$. Conversely, radiating energy permits contraction and raises the internal temperature. This refers to a sequence of gravitational equilibria, not to the positive local heat capacity of a parcel at fixed volume.

In an ordinary nondegenerate nuclear-burning star, a rise in nuclear heating tends to expand and cool the burning region, lowering its temperature-sensitive reaction rate. Reduced heating leads to contraction and reheating. This expansion feedback is the nuclear thermostat; it can fail in degenerate matter where pressure responds weakly to temperature. During contraction of a <pre-main-sequence star>, gravitational binding supplies the radiated <luminosity> and heats the interior. For a fixed-structure scaling, half the released gravitational energy increases $U$ and half escapes as radiation, until hydrogen burning can support a long-lived <main sequence> equilibrium.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Write $\Omega=-aGM_\odot^2/R_\odot$ with a structure factor $a$ of order unity. Then the thermal radiation timescale is
$$
\boxed{t_{\rm th}=\frac{U}{L_\odot}=\frac a2\frac{GM_\odot^2}{R_\odot L_\odot}\sim10^7\text{--}3\times10^7\,\mathrm{yr}.}
$$
For a uniform sphere $a=3/5$, this is about $9.4\times10^6$ years. The conventional <Kelvin-Helmholtz cooling time> $GM_\odot^2/(R_\odot L_\odot)$ is about $3.1\times10^7$ years; a centrally concentrated solar model changes the coefficient. Both estimates are far shorter than the nuclear lifetime.

Ionization and dissociation can store compression <energy> without the full temperature rise of a monatomic gas, lowering the effective adiabatic response in partially ionized outer layers. Radiation pressure tends towards an adiabatic exponent $4/3$. These effects, changing composition and degeneracy make the effective exponent position dependent. The local dynamical exponent is $\Gamma_1=(\partial\log P/\partial\log\rho)_s$; it equals $C_P/C_V$ in the simple fixed-composition ideal-gas model, but need not do so for an ionizing mixture.

If an envelope has an effective exponent at or below $4/3$, its pressure response is soft and sufficiently extensive such regions can promote radial pulsation, envelope instability or mass loss. In the ideal homologous global model, $\gamma=4/3$ is marginal and $\gamma<4/3$ is unstable to contraction or expansion. **A low value confined to a thin outer layer does not by itself prove collapse of the entire star**: global stability depends on the pressure-weighted structure. Its location and mass extent matter.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Displace a small parcel upward, with negligible heat exchange, rapid pressure balance, and the same composition as its surroundings. Define the environmental gradient $\nabla=d\log T/d\log P$. An adiabatic <ideal gas> parcel obeys $P\rho^{-\gamma}=\mathrm{constant}$ and hence $T\propto P^{(\gamma-1)/\gamma}$, giving $\nabla_{\rm ad}=(\gamma-1)/\gamma$.

The upward displacement has $\delta\log P<0$. If $\nabla<\nabla_{\rm ad}$, the parcel's temperature changes more negatively than the environment's. At their common <pressure> it is cooler, denser and driven back down. Therefore the <Schwarzschild criterion> for stability is
$$
\boxed{\nabla=\frac PT\frac{dT}{dP}<\nabla_{\rm ad}=\frac{\gamma-1}{\gamma}.}
$$
The reversed inequality produces buoyant growth, and equality is neutrally stable in this approximation. A composition gradient requires the <Ledoux criterion>; pressure equilibrium alone does not ensure equal mean molecular weight.

==== 5
{scope}

===== a
{scope}

====== Solution

In the Sun's cool outer layers, partial ionization lowers the adiabatic temperature gradient, while large opacity makes radiative transport inefficient. The required radiative gradient
$$
\nabla_{\rm rad}=\frac{3\kappa L_rP}{16\pi acGm_rT^4}
$$
can exceed $\nabla_{\rm ad}$, so <convection> carries part of the outward <luminosity>. The Sun therefore has an outer <convective envelope>, above its primarily radiative interior. Here $a$ is the radiation constant, $\kappa$ opacity per unit mass and $m_r,L_r$ the enclosed mass and luminosity. Near the visible surface, radiative losses are again important and the flow need not be perfectly adiabatic.

===== b
{scope}

====== Solution

Near a red giant's compact core, intense nuclear burning in a small region can produce a large $L_r/m_r$ and a steep required radiative gradient. Where this exceeds the adiabatic gradient, <convection> transports the released <energy> and mixes fuel. Core helium burning is a clear example: the strongly temperature-sensitive <triple-alpha process> commonly produces a convective burning core; shell flashes can also make convective regions.https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201527171[helium-burning core convection and its temperature-sensitive energy source]{ref}

The qualification matters: an inert degenerate helium core and a steady hydrogen-burning shell on the first-ascent <red giant branch> are not universally convective; conduction or radiation may dominate there. Red giants also commonly have deep <convective envelopes>. **The physical condition is an excessive required radiative gradient, not proximity to the centre alone.**

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

An <absorption line> depends on the population of its lower atomic level, ionization state, transition strength, pressure and broadening, not just elemental abundance. <Boltzmann distribution> changes excitation populations with <temperature>, while the <Saha equation> changes ionization with temperature and electron pressure. Thus similar overall composition can produce very different spectra. Molecules survive preferentially in cool atmospheres.

The <Harvard spectral classification> orders the main stellar classes by decreasing effective temperature: **O, B, A, F, G, K, M**, from blue to red. Numerical subclasses run approximately from 0, the hotter end of a class, to 9, its cooler end. An A0 star, near $10^4\,\mathrm K$, has very strong <Balmer series> lines; hotter stars ionize much of the hydrogen and cooler stars underpopulate its $n=2$ level. A G2 star, near $5800\,\mathrm K$, has weaker Balmer absorption, many neutral and ionized metal lines, prominent Ca II H and K lines and the CH G band. An M5 star, near $3000\,\mathrm K$, has strong TiO molecular bands, neutral-metal and alkali features, and weak photospheric Balmer absorption.https://voyages.sdss.org/expeditions/expedition-to-the-milky-way/spectral-types/absorption-and-emission-lines/[temperature sequence and characteristic atomic or molecular features]{ref}

For giant and dwarf stars of the same colour, compare gravity-sensitive line widths and ratios. The giant's larger radius gives lower surface gravity and atmospheric pressure, typically narrower pressure-broadened lines; high-pressure dwarf atmospheres have stronger broad alkali wings and different molecular and ionization ratios. These diagnostics assign a <luminosity class>, such as III for a giant and V for a dwarf. A line ratio or calibrated gravity indicator is stronger evidence than colour alone.

==== 2
{scope}

===== a
{scope}

====== Solution

A $1M_\odot$ star spends roughly $10^{10}$ years on the <main sequence>, deriving most of its <luminosity> from the <proton-proton chain>. After central hydrogen depletion, shell hydrogen burning around a contracting helium core expands it into a <red giant>. The low-mass helium core becomes electron-degenerate before the <helium flash>. Subsequent core helium burning by the <triple-alpha process>, with alpha capture producing oxygen, lasts of order $10^8$ years.

After central helium exhaustion, hydrogen and helium shells burn around a carbon-oxygen core on the <asymptotic giant branch>. Strong mass loss removes the envelope, potentially making a <planetary nebula>, and the remnant becomes a carbon-oxygen <white dwarf>. It cools using stored thermal energy and does not ignite sustained carbon burning. Detailed durations depend on composition and mass loss; **the nuclear endpoint of the isolated solar-mass model is a white dwarf**.

===== b
{scope}

====== Solution

A $5M_\odot$ star has a much greater luminosity and exhausts central hydrogen in roughly $10^8$ to $2\times10^8$ years, mainly through the <CNO cycle>. It crosses the <Hertzsprung gap>, develops shell hydrogen burning and ignites core helium without the strongly degenerate helium flash of the solar-mass case. Core helium burning lasts roughly $10^7$ years, with possible blue-loop evolution depending on the model.

It subsequently develops a carbon-oxygen core and double-shell burning on the <asymptotic giant branch>, with thermal pulses, winds and envelope loss. For ordinary isolated evolution at this mass, the core does not reach sustained carbon ignition and the final remnant is a carbon-oxygen <white dwarf>. The broad boundary between white-dwarf and core-collapse outcomes depends on composition, overshooting, rotation and mass loss; the quoted mass is comfortably in the usual intermediate-mass regime.

===== c
{scope}

====== Solution

A $15M_\odot$ star burns core hydrogen chiefly by the <CNO cycle> for about $10^7$ years. It commonly evolves towards a <red supergiant>, though winds and mixing alter its track. Core helium burning lasts of order $10^6$ years. Continued contraction then permits carbon, neon, oxygen and silicon burning, producing successively heavier nuclei up to an iron-group core. Neutrino cooling makes the advanced phases much shorter: carbon burning can last thousands of years, while the final burning stages fall to years or days, with substantial model dependence.

An iron-group core cannot gain energy by ordinary fusion into heavier nuclei. Electron captures, photodisintegration and loss of pressure support lead to <stellar core collapse>. A <core-collapse supernova> can eject the envelope and leave a <neutron star>; strong fallback or a failed explosion can instead produce a <black hole>. **The massive-star endpoint involves core collapse, rather than the quiet carbon-oxygen white-dwarf cooling of the two lower-mass examples.**

==== 3
{scope}

===== i
{scope}

====== Solution

For a young <open cluster>, the <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram> contains a populated <main sequence> reaching hot, luminous, high-mass stars, with a high-mass <main-sequence turnoff> determined by its age. Cool low-mass members may still lie on tracks of <pre-main-sequence stars> above the lower main sequence. Depending on how young the cluster is, a few evolved blue or red supergiants may already be present; an extremely young cluster need not have any. It lacks the dominant old low-mass red-giant and horizontal-branch populations of an old globular cluster.

\Image[2012-s26-cluster-hr-diagrams.png]
{title=Schematic temperature-luminosity diagrams of young and old star clusters}
{description=Temperature decreases to the right. The young cluster reaches a hot luminous turnoff and contains possible pre-main-sequence stars; the old cluster has a cool low-mass turnoff, red giant and horizontal branches, and a white-dwarf sequence. Curves are illustrative population sketches, not fitted isochrones.}

===== ii
{scope}

====== Solution

An old <globular cluster> has lost its original massive main-sequence members. Its <main-sequence turnoff> lies near a low stellar mass, typically around $0.8M_\odot$ for an age of order $10^{10}$ years. The diagram shows a lower <main sequence>, a <subgiant branch>, a prominent <red giant branch>, a <horizontal branch> of core-helium-burning stars, an <asymptotic giant branch> and a faint <white dwarf> cooling sequence. The preceding schematic labels these features. A few <blue stragglers> may lie above the old turnoff because interaction or merger rejuvenated them; they do not imply a young age for the bulk population.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume homologous, gas-pressure-supported <main sequence> stars with nearly constant composition and mean molecular weight $\mu$, radiative energy transport and approximately constant opacity $\kappa$. Hydrostatic and mass scalings give
$$
P_c\sim\frac{GM^2}{R^4},\qquad \rho_c\sim\frac M{R^3},\qquad T_c\sim\frac{\mu m_uGM}{k_{\rm B}R}.
$$
The <radiative diffusion> equation gives the luminosity scaling
$$
L\sim\frac{ac}{\kappa}\frac{RT_c^4}{\rho_c}\propto\frac{\mu^4}{\kappa}M^3.
$$
Therefore, under these explicit assumptions,
$$
\boxed{L\propto M^k,\qquad k=3.}
$$
The radius cancels. Radiation-pressure dominance in the most massive stars changes the relation towards the <Eddington luminosity> scaling $L\propto M$, so the cubic law is an idealized gas-pressure homology result rather than an exact universal massive-star law.

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

The trigonometric <parallax> gives $d=1/0.02=50\,\mathrm{pc}$. Neglecting extinction, the <distance modulus> and given <bolometric correction> give
$$
M_V=5.3-5\log_{10}(50/10)=1.80515,\qquad M_{\rm bol}=M_V-0.1=1.70515.
$$
Using the specified solar bolometric magnitude,
$$
\boxed{\frac L{L_\odot}=10^{0.4(4.6-1.70515)}\simeq14.39,\qquad
\frac M{M_\odot}\simeq(14.39)^{1/3}\simeq2.43.}
$$
The mass estimate uses the cubic scaling normalized to solar values. A lifetime estimate additionally needs the fraction of fuel accessible to core burning. Take a core-accessible fraction $f_c=0.1$ and initial hydrogen mass fraction $X=0.7$. The supplied fusion energy gives
$$
q_H=\frac{26.7\,\mathrm{MeV}}{4m_p}\simeq6.4\times10^{14}\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}},\qquad
\boxed{t_{\rm age}\sim\frac{f_cXMq_H}{L}\simeq1.25\times10^9\,\mathrm{yr}.}
$$
The alternative common solar-lifetime normalization $t\simeq10^{10}(M/M_\odot)/(L/L_\odot)$ years gives $1.69\times10^9$ years. Thus this simplified turnoff argument gives an age of order $10^9$ years, with its fuel fraction and mass-luminosity assumptions explicitly stated. It does not use a modern fitted Hyades age or a full stellar isochrone. Unresolved binaries, extinction and deviations from the homology law affect the estimate.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== a
{scope}

====== Solution

Face-on, the <Milky Way> is a <barred spiral galaxy>: a central bulge and bar of scale a few kiloparsecs lie inside a disk of stellar radius roughly $15\,\mathrm{kpc}$, with spiral arms containing gas, dust, <H II regions> and young stars. Recent <star formation> is concentrated in molecular clouds and arm complexes. The <Sun> lies in the disk about $8\,\mathrm{kpc}$ from the centre in the source's convention. Old stars are also present throughout the disk, while the bulge, thick disk and extended stellar halo contain important old populations. <Globular clusters> occupy a roughly spheroidal halo extending well beyond the bright disk.

\Image[2012-s26-milky-way-structure.png]
{title=Original schematic face-on and edge-on views of the Milky Way}
{description=The face-on panel labels a barred centre, spiral star-forming regions, the Sun and an approximate 15 kpc bright-disk radius. The edge-on panel separates the thin dusty disk, thick disk, bulge and old halo/globular-cluster population. Halo extent is indicated schematically beyond the plotted field.}

===== b
{scope}

====== Solution

Edge-on, the bright <thin galactic disk> forms a narrow band, with a central bulge protruding above and below it and a thinner gas-and-dust lane along the midplane. Young stars and <H II regions> occupy a vertical scale of a few tenths of a kiloparsec or less; a broader old <thick disk> has scale of order a kiloparsec. These are approximate scale heights, not sharp boundaries. The old <stellar halo> and <globular clusters> form a much larger, low-surface-brightness spheroidal component, extending to many tens of kiloparsecs or more. The <dark matter halo> is inferred dynamically rather than directly visible in such a sketch. The preceding figure is a structural diagram, not an external photograph or a reconstruction of a particular survey.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Massive young stars emit ionizing ultraviolet radiation that creates <H II regions>. Recombination and radiative cascades produce <H-alpha> emission, including the $n=3\to2$ transition. Shocks or other ionizing sources can also produce it, but disk star-forming gas is the natural interpretation for an extended spiral-galaxy rotation measurement.

The systemic <redshift> and inclination of an intrinsically circular thin disk are
$$
\boxed{z=\frac{689.1-656.3}{656.3}\simeq0.04998,\qquad
\cos i=\frac{7.5}{30}=\frac14,\quad i\simeq75.52^\circ.}
$$
The rotation-induced half-width is $(689.7-688.5)/2=0.6\,\mathrm{nm}$. Divide by the systemic observed wavelength to remove the common redshift factor before applying the small-velocity <Doppler shift>:
$$
v_{\rm los}=c\frac{0.6}{689.1}\simeq261.0\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\boxed{v_{\max}=\frac{v_{\rm los}}{\sin i}\simeq269.6\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
The circular-orbit and thin-disk assumptions neglect intrinsic thickness, warps and noncircular gas motion.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the low-redshift <Hubble law>, neglecting peculiar velocity and cosmological distance corrections:
$$
\boxed{d\simeq\frac{cz}{H_0}\simeq211\,\mathrm{Mpc},\qquad
R\simeq d\frac{15}{206265}\simeq15.35\,\mathrm{kpc}.}
$$
The angular radius is $15$ arcseconds, half the stated major-axis diameter. A spherical dynamical estimate gives
$$
\boxed{M(<R)\simeq\frac{v_{\max}^2R}{G}\simeq2.59\times10^{11}M_\odot.}
$$
It estimates mass inside the observed disk radius, not the entire unobserved halo. Disk geometry changes the exact coefficient.

Using the same approximate distance for the <distance modulus>,
$$
M_V=16-5\log_{10}\left(\frac{211\times10^6\,\mathrm{pc}}{10\,\mathrm{pc}}\right)\simeq-20.62,
$$
$$
\boxed{\frac{L_V}{L_{V,\odot}}=10^{0.4(4.8-M_V)}\simeq1.47\times10^{10},\qquad
\frac{M_{\rm dyn}}{L_V}\simeq17.6\frac{M_\odot}{L_{V,\odot}}.}
$$
For an illustrative stellar <mass-to-light ratio> of $1$--$5$ in solar $V$-band units, $M_*\sim1.5\times10^{10}$--$7.4\times10^{10}M_\odot$, below the dynamical estimate. This suggests substantial nonluminous matter under the adopted assumptions. Gas, stellar-population uncertainty, inclination, extinction and geometry also affect the comparison; luminosity alone cannot uniquely fix a stellar mass. The single-distance approximation at $z\simeq0.05$ is the intended introductory estimate.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Use a <Tully-Fisher relation> $L\propto v_{\max}^4$. A simple justification assumes comparable mean surface brightness, so $L\propto R^2$, and comparable total <mass-to-light ratio>, so $M\propto L$. The virial scaling $v^2\sim GM/R$ then gives $v^4\propto M^2/R^2\propto L$. Calibrating to the previous galaxy yields
$$
\boxed{L_2\simeq1.47\times10^{10}\left(\frac{100}{269.6}\right)^4L_{V,\odot}\simeq2.79\times10^8L_{V,\odot}.}
$$
This is a rough luminosity estimate: the empirical slope depends on passband and population, and the assumed constant surface brightness and mass-to-light ratio are approximations rather than exact properties of every spiral galaxy.

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

For a spherical <mass distribution> with constant circular <speed> $v_c$,
$$
M(<r)=\frac{v_c^2r}{G},\qquad
\boxed{\rho(r)=\frac{1}{4\pi r^2}\frac{dM}{dr}=\frac{v_c^2}{4\pi Gr^2}.}
$$
For $v_c=220\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$, this is about $0.0140(r/8\,\mathrm{kpc})^{-2}M_\odot\,\mathrm{pc^{-3}}$. It is the spherical model's total density; it is singular if extrapolated to the very centre and is intended only over the flat-rotation region.

For a sharp outer truncation at $r_*$, the exterior mass is fixed at $M_*=v_c^2r_*/G$. Integrating the gravitational work from $r$ to the boundary and then to infinity gives
$$
\frac12v_{\rm esc}^2=\int_r^{r_*}\frac{v_c^2}{s}\,ds+\int_{r_*}^\infty\frac{GM_*}{s^2}\,ds
=v_c^2\left[\log\frac{r_*}{r}+1\right].
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{v_{\rm esc}(r)=\sqrt{2}v_c\sqrt{1+\log(r_*/r)}.}
$$
The exterior contribution cannot be dropped: at $r=r_*$ it correctly gives the point-mass escape speed $\sqrt2v_c$.

If the fastest measured radial speed is assumed to approximate both the total speed and the local escape speed of a bound star, then at $r=8\,\mathrm{kpc}$,
$$
\boxed{r_*\simeq r\exp\left(\frac{430^2}{2(220)^2}-1\right)\simeq19.9\,\mathrm{kpc}.}
$$
Without those extra assumptions, the measurement gives only a bound: a bound star's total speed is at least its radial component, and the sample maximum need not reach escape, so $r_*\gtrsim19.9\,\mathrm{kpc}$ within this model. An unbound interloper would invalidate even that bound. This is not a determination of the real Milky Way halo edge.
